%    Allowing machine learning models to abstain from predicting is a well-established strategy for mitigating prediction errors in high-stakes scenarios. While extensive research has focused on abstention in classification tasks, its application in ranking remains under-explored despite the critical role of ranking in decision-making contexts. This paper advances the bounded-abstention paradigm, characterized by a predefined limit on abstentions, into the domain of pairwise learning-to-rank. In particular, we develop a method for abstention in pairwise learning-to-rank settings, by characterizing the optimal selection strategy, which specifies when the ranker should refrain from providing a relative order of certain elements. More precisely, such an optimal strategy relies on thresholding the ranker's conditional risk and opting for abstention when the conditional risk is higher than the threshold. Our approach enables models to strategically abstain on pairwise ordering relationships, ensuring higher accuracy on the asserted orders, while allowing for further investigations or human intervention on the uncertain part of the ranking. Through extensive experiments on benchmark datasets, we illustrate the value of abstention as a powerful tool for enhancing the accuracy of ranking models in real-world applications.
