In other words, $f$ incurs a loss of one whenever the pair $(\mbx,\mbx')$ is ranked by  $f$ differently from the ground truth order; if the ranking is correct, no loss is incurred.
Let $P$ be a probability distribution over $\mcX \times \mcX \times \mcY$. We denote with $R_{l}(f) = \mathbb{E}_{(\mbX,\mbX', Y)\sim P}[l\left(f (\mbX, \mbX'), Y\right)]$
the expected $l$ loss of the ranker $f$. Typically, such a loss is not directly tractable. Hence, a few approaches propose to learn scores $s:\mcX\to\mathbb{R}$ that provide a value for the relevance of the single item $\mbx$. Generally, these scores are learnt through (stochastic) gradient descent and boosting techniques, e.g., Ranknet, LambdaRank and LambdaMart \citep{burges2010ranknet}.
Given $s$, one can estimate
