P(Y=1\mid \mbX=\mbx, \mbX'=\mbx')$, i.e., the probability that $\mbx$ is ranked higher than $\mbx'$, $P(Y=-1\mid \mbX=\mbx, \mbX'=\mbx')$, i.e., the probability that $\mbx$ is ranked lower than $\mbx'$, and $P(Y=0\mid \mbX=\mbx, \mbX'=\mbx')$, i.e., the two instances are tied, through parametric probability models~\citep{zhou2008learning}. The two most famous approaches are the \textit{Bradley-Terry model}~\citep{bradley1952rank} and the
\textit{Thurstone-Mosteller model }~\citep{thurstone1994law} (for tie-adjusted models, see, e.g., \cite{zhou2008learning}).
The former estimates probabilities as follows:
