%\end{equation}
However, %Learning to Reject has a couple of main drawbacks: first, 
determining the value of $a$ is context-dependent and difficult to determine \citep{denis2020consistency,DBLP:conf/aaai/RuggieriP25}. Moreover, there is no \textit{ex-ante} control on the fraction of abstentions, which is a critical requirement in practice. 
%
Selective Prediction methods solve those concerns by controlling the trade-off between performance and abstention through the \textit{coverage} $\phi(g) = \mathbb{E}[g(\mbX)]$~\citep{DBLP:journals/jmlr/El-YanivW10}, namely the probability of acceptance. 
In the bounded-abstention formulation%\footnote{Another way to frame the goal is through the bounded-improvement model, where one aims to maximize coverage given a limited maximum risk.}
~\citep{DBLP:journals/jmlr/FrancPV23}, this translates into the following objective:
\begin{equation}
    \argmin_{(f,g)} %_{\theta\in\Theta, \psi\in\Psi} 
    \frac{\mathbb{E}_{\mbX,Y\sim P}[l\left(f(\mbX),Y \right)g(\mbX)]}{\phi(g)} \ \ \text{s.t.} \ \ \phi(g)\geq c,
\end{equation}
where $c$ is the \textit{target coverage}, i.e., the user-defined fraction of cases for which the selective predictor $(f, g)$ \textit{must} provide a prediction. Theoretical results can be found in \citet{DBLP:journals/jmlr/FrancPV23} for the classification task and in \citet{DBLP:conf/nips/ZaouiDH20} for the regression task.
