To the best of our knowledge, in the setting of ranking, only the following approaches have been proposed: \citet{cheng2010predicting,DBLP:conf/nips/ChengHWW12} study the problem of abstention, in particular for the label ranking setting, with the selective predictors returning partial orders (instead of total orders) on the labels;  \citet{Mao23} have considered pairwise ranking with abstention in the framework of Learning to Reject, introducing a cost for abstaining on a pair of instances. 
As for classification, such an approach hinders a practical implementation because specifying the correct cost for abstaining on a pair is not straightforward, making the cost-based model and the bounded-abstention approaches not directly comparable~\citep{Benchmark2024}.
