These results confirm the effectiveness of our approach in satisfying the coverage constraint at test time.


\paragraph{Q3: evaluating the acceptance ratios}


A critical aspect often underestimated in the literature, is the ability of abstaining systems to keep stable distribution of classes $y \in \mcY = \{-1,0,1\}$ in the selected (pairs of) instances. %, where $-1$ means that the second instance has a higher relevance than the first, $1$ indicates that the first is preferred and $0$ a tie). 
Abstention that concentrates on one class introduces forms of biases in the decision-making process~\citep{Benchmark2024}. 
Overall, all baselines show stable $SelRate$ when varying $c$.
\cref{fig:SRAres} (and \cref{fig:cat_res_SRA_BALToR,fig:lgbm_res_SRA_BALToR} in \cref{Appendix:additionalresults}) provide results for the three datasets, when considering \BT{} paired with \BALToR{}. For the sake of space, we provide the results for the entropy baseline and \TM{} in \cref{Appendix:additionalresults} (the random baseline is stable by definition). 



On \texttt{MQ2007}, when considering the \BT{} model, the $SelRate$ for the $0-$class pairs ranges between $\approx .718\pm .005$ and $\approx.725\pm.005$, the $-1$-class $SelRate$ is between $\approx.143\pm.007$ and $\approx .138  \pm.007$ and the $-1$-class $SelRate$ is stable around $\approx .139\pm.007$ and $\approx .136\pm .007$. 

