%Le modèle de sécurité réseau basé sur la segmentation repose sur une confiance implicite au sein d'un segment. Cette vulnérabilité est exacerbée par l'utilisation intensive des VPN. Le modèle de confiance zero trust (ZT) évite cette confiance implicite en appliquant le principe \textit{« ne jamais faire confiance, toujours vérifier »}, avec une vérification continue de la confiance, une authentification et un cryptage. Les organisations adoptent de plus en plus les architectures ZT pour une meilleure cybersécurité. Pour sécuriser des systèmes entiers, elles doivent connaître les attaques et recueillir des informations sur les cybermenaces (CTI). La CTI diffuse des informations à différents niveaux de confidentialité et les intègre dans les ontologies STIX. Évaluer la confiance dans l'information combine plusieurs dimensions. Cela nécessite un formalisme pour agréger des informations incertaines de diverses sources qu’on a implémenté pour faire des tests en utilisant le cadre d'Adrien et Marie-Jeanne \cite{Ch9} \cite{DBLP:conf/ipmu/dAllonnesL14} \cite{RevaultLesot2015}.
\begin{abstract}

Le renseignement sur les cybermenaces (\textit{Cyber Threat Intelligence}, ou CTI) est essentiel en cyberdéfense. Il recense les menaces qui peuvent peser sur un système informatique. Ces informations sont collectées par un agent par observation, ou en les recevant de sources. Il est nécessaire d'estimer la confiance dans chaque information acquise, en tenant compte des différentes dimensions qui peuvent composer la confiance : fiabilité de la source, compétence, plausibilité de l'information, crédibilité de l'information, par exemple. Ces informations recueillies doivent ensuite être agrégées pour consolider les renseignements. Des progrès ont été réalisés dans la théorie qui sous-tend la modélisation de la confiance quand elle est multidimensionnelle et que potentiellement certaines valeurs des dimensions sont indéterminées. Nous présentons ici la problématique du CTI et le partage de renseignements, et les raisons pour lesquelles nous utilisons une logique pour une première implémentation.
\end{abstract}

%{Confiance, logique multivaluée, cybersécurité, Threat Intelligence (TI).}
%{
%The widely used segmentation-based network security model relies on implicit trust within a segment, then traffic is not monitored. This implicit trust is a major vulnerability nowadays, as intensive VPN usage allows external devices to directly access a specific segment. The Zero Trust (ZT) model avoids such implicit trust by operating on the principle of \textit{« never trust, always verify »}. It mandates continuous verification of trust for all users, devices, and communication flows, and imposes the standard use of strong authentication and encryption. Organizations are increasingly adopting ZT architectures for better cybersecurity. To secure entire systems they must know attacks and gather Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI). CTI disseminates information at varying confidentiality levels and integrates it into STIX ontologies. Evaluating trust in information involves considering various dimensions. This requires a formalism to aggregate uncertain information from various sources, which we implemented for testing purposes using Adrien and Marie-Jeanne's framework \cite{Ch9} \cite{DBLP:conf/ipmu/dAllonnesL14} \cite{RevaultLesot2015}. 
%Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) is one of the key elements of an efficient defense of a system. An agent in charge of intelligence usually relies on collecting information from different sources. The threat intelligence process requires estimating the trust one can have in each piece of information by considering various dimensions related to the trust. Information freshly gathered has to be aggregated with other information to consolidate an agent's knowledge. Recent progress has been made in the theory behind modeling trust and decision-making, notably by using multivalued logics. This framework provides interesting features, such as the possibility to hand unknown values for trust-building parameters, or the seamless integration of dimensions. In this paper, we present our work, consisting of implementing a trust-building multivalued logic framework based on the state-of-the-art, and estimating the relevance of this model for handling the decision-making process for cyber threat intelligence.
%Cyber threat intelligence (CTI) is essential for effective system defense. CTI is a collection of information about current or past threats to a computer system. This information is gathered by an agent through observation, or based on a set of sources. Building intelligence only makes sense if you have confidence in it. To achieve this, it is necessary to estimate the confidence in each piece of information gathered, taking into account the different dimensions that can make it up: reliability of the source, competence, plausibility of the information, credibility of the information, for example. The information gathered must then be combined with other information to consolidate an agent's knowledge. Recent advances have been made in the theory underlying the modeling of trust for decision-making based on uncertain information, notably by using multivalued logic. This approach makes it possible to deal with unknown values of trust-building parameters, or to easily integrate dimensions. In this article we present the problem of CTI and CTI information sharing, and the reasons that led us to use a logic-based solution for an initial implementation.

%Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) is essential to cyber defense. It identifies potential threats to a computer system. This information is gathered by an agent through observation, or by using sources. It is then necessary to estimate the confidence in each piece of information gathered, taking into account the various dimensions that can make up confidence: reliability of the source, competence, plausibility of the information, credibility of the information, for example. The information gathered must then be aggregated to consolidate it. Progress has been made in the theory underlying the modeling of trust when it is multidimensional and potentially some of the values of the dimensions are indeterminate. Here, we present the problem of CTI and intelligence sharing, and the reasons why we are using a logic for a first implementation.
