\begin{abstract}
This paper investigates the design of output-feedback schemes for systems described by a class of recurrent neural networks. We propose a procedure based on linear matrix inequalities for designing an observer and a static state-feedback controller. The algorithm leverages global and regional incremental input-to-state stability ($\delta$ISS) and enables the tracking of constant setpoints, ensuring robustness to disturbances and state estimation uncertainty. To address the potential limitations of regional $\delta$ISS, we introduce an alternative scheme in which the static law is replaced with a tube-based nonlinear model predictive controller (NMPC) that exploits regional $\delta$ISS properties. We show that these conditions enable the formulation of a robust NMPC law with guarantees of convergence and recursive feasibility, leading to an enlarged region of attraction. Theoretical results are validated through numerical simulations on the pH-neutralisation process benchmark, demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed schemes.
\end{abstract}
\begin{keywords}
Recurrent neural networks, incremental sector condition, nonlinear model predictive control, robust control, output-feedback control, incremental input-to-state stability
\end{keywords}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}
In the last decades, the increased availability of plant measurements \cite{wu2008top} has led to growing interest in data-based control approaches \cite{tang2022data}. In particular, within the framework of indirect data-based control, recurrent neural network (RNN) models have gained attention for their ability to accurately approximate nonlinear system dynamics. Leveraging their modelling capabilities, control practitioners are investigating the use of RNN models identified from input-output data for the synthesis of model-based control strategies \cite{bonassi2022recurrent}.

Within this framework, a widespread approach to model-based design involves constraining the RNN training procedure to ensure that the identified model satisfies specific stability properties (such as input-to-state stability (ISS) \cite{jiang2001input} or incremental ISS ($\delta$ISS) \cite{bayer2013discrete}), which simplifies the control design. This approach has been applied, for instance, to the development of nonlinear model predictive control (NMPC) algorithms \cite{terzi2021learning,bonassi2021nonlinear,bonassi2024nonlinear,schimperna2024robust}, internal model control (IMC) architectures \cite{furieri2024learning,bonassi2022recurrent_imc}, and state observers \cite{bonassi2024nonlinear,schimperna2024robust} with proven theoretical guarantees. Regarding state observers, note that incremental input/output-to-state stability provides a form of detectability for nonlinear systems \cite{allan2021nonlinear}. 
However, this approach suffers from some limitations. Firstly, enforcing sufficient and possibly conservative conditions during model identification can restrict the set of admissible parameter values, possibly excluding parameters that could accurately describe the system dynamics. Secondly, this approach is not applicable when the system to be controlled does not exhibit the desired open-loop properties.\\ Therefore, it is important to study new conditions to impart these properties to the RNN-based control system. %Therefore, it is important to define conditions for designing feedback controllers that enforce these properties in closed-loop.\\
Note that, closed-loop $\delta$ISS is crucial for the development of robust controllers. For example, it enables the definition of robustly positive invariant (RPI) sets \cite{bayer2013discrete}, which are essential in the design of tube-based NMPC schemes \cite{mayne2000constrained} and which, in the output-feedback framework, are essential to develop integrated design procedures that explicitly account for potential state estimation uncertainty \cite{shamma1998output,mayne2006robust}. \\
Only a few studies investigate the design of RNN-based output-feedback control schemes \cite{vance2008discrete,yin2021stability,seel2021neural,d2023incremental,ravasio2024lmi,la2025regional}. However, most of these works focus on equilibrium stability, while only \cite{d2023incremental,ravasio2024lmi} investigate design conditions for enforcing closed-loop $\delta$ISS. In particular, the work \cite{d2023incremental} derives design conditions based on linear matrix inequalities (LMIs) \cite{boyd1994linear} for a general model class encompassing various RNN architectures, such as echo state networks and neural nonlinear autoregressive exogenous models. This work is then extended in \cite{ravasio2024lmi}, where an LMI-based procedure for the design of a robust NMPC controller with guaranteed convergence and recursive feasibility is proposed.
\\
The main limitation of the cited approaches is that they aim to enforce global properties to the control system. However, global $\delta$ISS is a strong property that may not always be admissible, for example, due to the nonlinearities of the RNN model. Nevertheless, global stability notions are often unnecessary in practice, as physical systems typically operate within bounded regions due to saturation limits. To address this limitation, local conditions for the design of stabilizing controllers should be investigated.\\
For the case of linear systems subject to saturation limits, LMI conditions have been derived in \cite{massimetti2009linear} for the design of anti-windup control systems with regional stability and performance. This approach has been extended in \cite{la2025regional} to the control of a class of RNN models. %A key aspect of defining local conditions for RNNs is the description of the nonlinearities within the RNN model using a sector condition.
However, these studies primarily focus on equilibrium stability, and, to the best of the authors' knowledge, no prior work has addressed the problem of formulating conditions for designing controllers that enforce regional $\delta$ISS to the closed-loop system.

In this work, we consider the control of a plant described by an RNN model, subject to disturbances affecting the state dynamics and measurement noise. Specifically, we focus on a subclass of recurrent equilibrium networks (REN) \cite{revay2023recurrent}, as defined in \cite{la2025regional}. We derive an LMI-based procedure for designing an output-feedback law consisting of a state observer and a static state-feedback controller, ensuring global or regional closed-loop $\delta$ISS. The key result in formulating these conditions is the introduction of a novel incremental sector condition for a class of nonlinear functions commonly used in RNN models.\\ However, when these properties hold only locally, the feasibility region of the controller may be limited. To address this potential limitation, we show that the static law within the output-feedback scheme can be seamlessly replaced with a tube-based NMPC law by incorporating additional LMIs into the design procedure. NMPC significantly enlarges the controller's feasibility region while accommodating the inclusion of input and output constraints.
\smallskip\\
The rest of the paper is organised as follows. Section~\ref{sec:problem_statement} introduces the RNN model, the control objective and the incremental sector condition. The design conditions for the observer, static state-feedback law and NMPC law are presented in Sections~\ref{sec:observer}, \ref{sec:local_dISS}, and \ref{sec:MPC}, respectively. Simulation results are provided in Section~\ref{sec:simulations}. Conclusions are drawn in Section~\ref{sec:conclusions}. The proofs of the main results are reported in the Appendix.
