\subsection{The control system}
We now consider the control system depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:control_architecture}, which integrates
the observer dynamics~\eqref{eq:observer_dynamics} with the control law~\eqref{eq:control_law_tracking}. To provide stability properties, the observer gains $(L, \tilde{L})$ and the control gain $K$ must be designed according to Theorems~\ref{th:dISS_observer} and \ref{th:dISS}, respectively.\\
Note, however, that conditions \eqref{eq:LMIs_observer} and \eqref{eq:LMIs} are not LMIs and the solution could result numerically complex.  
To avoid this, we propose the LMI-based procedure described in Algorithm~\ref{alg:design_procedure}.\\
The proposed procedure consists of two main steps. The first part aims to compute the observer gains and the associated RPI set $\mathcal{E}(P_o)/\gamma_o$ based on Theorem~\ref{th:dISS_observer}. Since \eqref{eq:LMIs_observer} is not an LMI problem, we make an initial guess for $H_o$ and $\gamma_o$ and solve the restricted LMI problem \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_obs}-\eqref{eq:locality_set_dISS_obs1} with decision variables $P_o$, $Q_{o,x}$, $Q_{o,w}$, $N$, $\tilde{N}$, and $S_o$. This provides an initial partial solution. Then, by fixing $S_o$, we solve \eqref{eq:LMIs_observer} as an LMI problem to compute the final solution.  If the problem is infeasible, $H_o$ and $\gamma_o$ are updated iteratively (see steps \(5\) and \(7\)) until a feasible solution is found. Note that, as an alternative to solving \eqref{eq:LMIs_observer} in step $14$, it is possible to scale the set $\mathcal{E}(P_o / \gamma_o)$ by reducing $\gamma_o$ until  
$|\tilde{L}_i C e + \tilde{L} \eta| \leq \bar{v}_i(h_{o,i})$, for all $(e,\eta) \in \mathcal{E}(P_o / \gamma_o)\times \mathcal{E}(Q_\eta^0)$, and $i \in \{1, \dots, \nu\}$.\\
The second part of Algorithm~\ref{alg:design_procedure} starts making an initial guess for $H_c$ and $\gamma_c$ and tries to solve the LMI problem \eqref{eq:dISS_condition}-\eqref{eq:locality_set_dISS_ctrobs2}  with decision variables $Q_c$, $\tilde{Q}_{c,x}$, $Q_{c,w}$, $Z$, and $S_c$, to determine the control gain and the associated RPI set $\mathcal{E}(P_c)/\gamma_c \oplus \bar{x}$.  If the problem is infeasible, $H_c$ and $\gamma_c$ are updated iteratively (see Steps $21$ and $23$) until a feasible solution is found.\\
To ensure that the solution exists in the largest possible region, we initialize $H_c = I_\nu$ and $H_o = I_\nu$ and attempt to solve \eqref{eq:LMIs} (resp., \eqref{eq:LMIs_observer}) by incrementally increasing $\gamma_c$ (resp., $\gamma_o$). If no feasible solution is found, we search for a local solution by updating $H_c$ and/or $H_o$. Note, however, that if the disturbances are too large, no solution may exist for the selected setpoint.\\
Finally, note that, as we aim to maximise the region of attraction $\mathcal{E}(P_c/\gamma_c)$ of the controller (resp., minimise the estimation error set $\mathcal{E}(P_o/\gamma_o)$), we recommend complementing the LMI problem \eqref{eq:dISS_condition}-\eqref{eq:locality_set_dISS_ctrobs2} in step $19$ (resp., \eqref{eq:LMIs_observer} in step $13$) with a cost function $f_c(Q_c)$ (resp., $f_o(P_o)$). These functions should be chosen by the designer to appropriately shape the RPI sets. A more detailed discussion on possible function choices can be found in \cite{boyd1994linear}.
%    \step{0} Set $H_o = I_\nu$, $\gamma_o = 1$, $H_c = I_\nu$, and $\gamma_c = 1$, and define small scalars $\epsilon_h$ and $\epsilon_\gamma$.\\  
%    \step{1} Compute $\bar{v}_i(h_{o,i})$ for $i = 1, \dots, \nu$ by solving \eqref{opt:locality_constraints}, and solve the LMI problem \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_obs}-\eqref{eq:locality_set_dISS_obs1} with unknowns $P_o$, $Q_{o,x}$, $Q_{o,{w_o}}$, $S_o$, $N$, and $\tilde N$. If a solution exists, fix $S_o$ and go to Step~\textit{2}. Otherwise, update either $H_o = H_o + \epsilon_h I_\nu$ or $\gamma_o = \gamma_o + \epsilon_\gamma$, and repeat Step~\textit{1}.\\  
