\section{MPC control design}\label{sec:MPC}
Theorem~\ref{th:dISS}, discussed in Section \ref{sec:local_dISS}, provides a condition for the design of a static output-feedback control law \eqref{eq:control_law_tracking} for tracking the setpoint triple $(\bar x,\bar u,\bar y)$.
A potential weakness of the control system described in the previous section is related to the local $\delta$ISS property: indeed, convergence is ensured when the initial conditions lie in suitable, but possibly small, RPI sets, which may limit the applicability of the controller around the selected nominal operating conditions. To overcome this potential problem, in this section we design a suitable MPC controller that uses \eqref{eq:control_law_tracking} as an auxiliary control law; in this case the invariant set computed in the previous section just serves as a terminal feasible set, potentially greatly enlarging the region of attraction of the setpoint.\smallskip\\
%
As customary in the MPC framework, in this section we assume that the system operation must be subject to input and output constraints, i.e. $(u,y)\in\mathbb U\times\mathbb Y$ and we consider the following.
\begin{ass}\label{ass:compact_sets}
    The sets $\mathbb U$ and $\mathbb Y$ are polytopes, i.e., $\mathbb{U}=\{u\in\R^m:G_{u}u\leq b_{u}\}$, where $G_u\in\R^{n_s\times m}$ and $b_u\in\R^{n_s}$, and $\mathbb{Y}=\{y\in\R^p:G_{y}y\leq b_{y}\}$, where $G_y\in\R^{n_r\times p}$ and $b_y\in\R^{n_r}$. \hfill{}$\square$
\end{ass}
%
%
To address the stated control objectives, we propose the NMPC output-feedback control architecture represented in Figure \ref{fig:control_architecture_MPC}, which consists of the nonlinear observer~\eqref{eq:observer_dynamics}, designed based on Theorem~\ref{th:dISS_observer} and a tube-based state-feedback NMPC law.
%
%
\subsection{The nominal prediction model}
%As in~\cite{mayne2006robust}, we directly control the observer dynamics \eqref{eq:observer_dynamics_2}.\\
The model used for computing the state predictions in the MPC-related optimization problem is obtained from \eqref{eq:observer_dynamics_2} by neglecting the uncertain terms depending on $\eta(k)$ and $e(k)$. The resulting nominal system dynamics is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:nom_system}
    \tilde x(k+1) = A\tilde x(k)+B\tilde u(k)+B_qq(\tilde A\tilde x(k)+\tilde B\tilde u(k)),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde x\in\R^n$ and $\tilde u\in\R^m$ represent the nominal state and nominal input vectors, respectively.\\
The input $\tilde u(k)$ and the state $\tilde x(k)$ will be used to compute the real plant input through the following control law
\begin{equation}\label{eq:control_law_tube}
u(k)=\tilde u(k)+K\tilde e(k),
\end{equation}
where $\tilde e(k)=\hat x(k)-\tilde x(k)$, and the gain $K$ is selected according to the following proposition.
%
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\begin{figure}[tp]
    \fontsize{8}{12}\selectfont
    \centering
    \def\svgwidth{0.95\columnwidth}
    \input{control_architecture_MPC}
    \caption{NMPC output-feedback control architecture.}
    \label{fig:control_architecture_MPC}
\end{figure}
