\textbf{Proof of Lemma 
\ref{lem:NScond_obs}.}
To prove that the feasibility of~\eqref{eq:dISS_condition_obs} implies the detectability of $(A,C)$, observe that, since $N=P_oL$ and $\tilde N=S_o\tilde L$, inequality \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_obs} is equivalent to \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_2_obs}. Defining
\begin{equation}\label{eq:congr_transf}
T=\begin{bmatrix}
        I&0&0&0\\
        0&0&I&0\\
        0&0&0&I\\
        0&I&0&0
    \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
it follows from \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_2_obs} that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dISS_condition_3_obs}
    \begin{aligned}
&T^\top\begin{bmatrix}
    P_o-Q_{o,x}&-\tilde A_L^\top S_o &0&A_L^\top P_o\\
    -S_o\tilde A_L&2S_oH_o& -S_o\tilde D_o&B_q^\top P_o\\
    0&-\tilde D_o^\top S_o&Q_{o,w_o}&D_o^\top P_o\\
    P_oA_L&P_oB_q&P_oD_o&P_o
    \end{bmatrix}T\\
    &=\begin{bmatrix}
    P_o-Q_{o,x}&A_L^\top P_o&-\tilde A_L^\top S_o &0\\
    P_oA_L&P_o&P_oB_q&P_oD_o\\
    -S_o\tilde A_L&B_q^\top P_o&2S_oH_o& -S_o\tilde D_o\\
    0&D_o^\top P_o&-\tilde D_o^\top S_o&Q_{o,w_o}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \succeq 0.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Note that a necessary condition for \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_3_obs} is 
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    P_o-Q_{o,x}&A_L^\top P_o\\
    P_oA_L&P_o
    \end{bmatrix}\succeq0
\end{equation*}
which, given that $P_o$ is invertible by design, can be rewritten using the Schur complement as
$P_o-Q_{o,x}-A_L^\top P_oA_L\succeq 0$. This inequality holds if $A_L$ is a Schur matrix having quadratic Lyapunov function $V_o(x)=x^\top P_ox$. This is possible only if $(A,C)$ is detectable.\smallskip\\
%
We now prove that the detectability of $(A,C)$ implies the feasibility of~\eqref{eq:dISS_condition_obs}. Leveraging the Schur complement, note that condition \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_3_obs} can be rewritten as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dISS_condition_4_obs} 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    P_o-Q_{o,x}&A_L^\top P_o\\
    P_oA_L&P_o
    \end{bmatrix}-M_oQ_{H_ow_o}^{-1}M_o^\top\succeq0
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation*}
M_o=\begin{bmatrix}
    -\tilde A_L^\top S_o&0\\
    P_oB_q&P_oD_o
\end{bmatrix}, \ \ Q_{H_ow_o}=\begin{bmatrix}
    2S_oH_o&-S_o\tilde D\\
    -\tilde D^\top S_o&Q_{o,w_o}
\end{bmatrix}. 
\end{equation*}
Since $(A,C)$ is detectable, there exists $L$ such that $A_L=A-LC$ is Schur stable. In view of the Schureness of $A_L$, we can select $P_o$ and $Q_{o,x}$ such that 
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    P_o-Q_{o,x}&A_L^\top P_o\\
    P_oA_L&P_o
    \end{bmatrix}\succeq \bar lI,
\end{equation*}
where $\bar l\in\R_+$. This allows us to fulfill \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_4_obs} by setting
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dISS_condition_5_obs}
    \bar lI-M_o Q_{H_ow_o}^{-1}M_o^\top\succeq0.
\end{equation}
Condition \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_5_obs} is satisfied if, and only if, there exist matrices $S_o$, $H_o$ and $Q_{o,{w_o}}$ such that $\norm{M_o Q_{H_ow_o}^{-1} M_o^\top} \leq \bar l$. Furthermore, being $Q_{o,{w_o}}$ symmetric, then $Q_{H_ow_o}$ is also symmetric and has real eigenvalues. It follows that
\[\norm{M_oQ_{H_ow_o}^{-1}M_o^\top}\leq \norm{M_o}^2\norm{Q_{H_ow_o}^{-1}}\leq\cfrac{\norm{M_o}^2}{|\lambda_{min}(Q_{H_ow_o})|}.\]
Therefore, condition \eqref{eq:dISS_condition_5_obs} can be satisfied by imposing
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sufficient_condition_obs}
    |\lambda_{min}(Q_{H_ow_o})|\geq\cfrac{\norm{M_o}^2}{\bar l}.
\end{equation}
According to the Geršgorin theorem~\cite{Varga2004Gersgorin}, all eigenvalues of the matrix $Q_{H_ow_o} = [q_{ij}] \in \mathbb{R}^{\nu+d+p\times\nu+d+p}$ lie within the union of Geršgorin discs, which are centred at $q_{ii}$ with radii $r_i = \sum_{j \neq i} |q_{ij}|$, for all $i = 1, \dots, \nu+d+p$. By choosing, for instance, $S_o = \frac{1}{2} I_\nu$, $Q_{o,{w_o}} = \alpha I_{d+p}$ and $H_o = \alpha I_\nu$, where $\alpha\in\R_{\geq 0}$, we have $q_{ii} = \alpha$ for all $i=1,\dots,\nu+d$. Moreover, note that $\bar l$, $M_o$, and $r_i$ for $i = 1,\dots,\nu+d$ do not depend on $\alpha$, and
\[\lambda_{min}(Q_{H_ow_o})\geq\alpha-\max_{i=1,\dots,\nu+d}r_i.\] 
Therefore, increasing $\alpha$ effectively shifts the centres of the Geršgorin discs toward larger values along the real axis in the complex plane, increasing the eigenvalues of $Q_{H_ow_o}$. Therefore, condition \eqref{eq:sufficient_condition_obs}
can be satisfied by selecting $\alpha$ such that \[\alpha\geq\cfrac{\norm{M_o}^2}{\bar l}+\max_{i=1,\dots,\nu+d}r_i,\] 
thus completing the proof.
\hfill{}$\square$\smallskip\\
