\vspace*{1mm}
{\bf Machine learning for dynamic channel hopping.} Dynamic channel hopping adapts the channel hopping sequence in response to changes in channel quality and interference conditions. Typically, this is accomplished through proactive spectrum sensing and message exchange with neighboring nodes to obtain these measures. However, frequent message exchange among nodes introduces significant communication overhead, thereby decreasing throughput efficiency, especially in large or more mobile wireless networks. Scaling the overhead of obtaining up-to-date network states in dynamic networks remains a challenging issue. In this paper, we consider the alternative of eschewing explicit network state sharing, with an alternative approach that predicts network states in the presence of network dynamics using machine learning. 
%Mobile application development in IoT (e.g., 5G and mobile IoT) is a growing trend to connect mobile devices in dynamic networks. The growing throughput-latency demands of mobile applications and the considerations of mobility and interference have motivated the development of performance-efficient communication that can adapt to dynamic network environments. To achieve optimal channel-hopping scheduling in dynamic network topology, it is essential to avoid the staleness of the retrieved network states. Conventional MAC protocols exploit message exchange with the neighboring node in a proactive or reactive manner to obtain the current state of the network. However, frequent message exchange among nodes introduces significant communication overhead as the network size increases and therefore decreases the throughput efficiency, especially in capacity-limited multi-hop networks (e.g., satellite mesh networks and industrial IoT).

Empirical studies have shown that it is possible to learn link correlations from information observed at the network nodes. The work in \cite{li2020lstm, lee2020detection} shows that channel hopping sequences can be predicted from historical spectrum data using long short-term memory (LSTM) and convolutional neural (CNN) networks. Other work has demonstrated the feasibility of reverse engineering the channel hopping sequence by eavesdropping on unencrypted packet headers transmitted over networks \cite{cheng2019cracking, cheng2021launching}. However, these studies have not considered network dynamics, including node mobility or external interference, and their approach did not perform well in our preliminary experiments with dynamic networks. 

%Empirical studies have shown that it is possible to predict the correlation between nodes in dynamic networks. The problem of predicting relationships in networks is called link prediction. Link prediction aims to infer link information (e.g., channel used for the link at a given time) by predicting future relationships based on historical observed link states. In frequency hopping-based networks with node mobility, it is possible for a node to predict the future link activity within its neighborhood (i.e., the communication channels used in neighboring nodes at a given time slot) if there exist regular patterns of channel hopping sequences used in nodes and the mobility model is predictable. Since each node can collect historical link data by passively observing the channel occupancy state in the neighborhood, applying a prediction-based solution introduces zero communication overhead, thus achieving higher throughput efficiency compared to protocols relying on proactive or reactive beaconing. 

\vspace*{1mm}
\textbf{The MiLAAP\footnote{"Milaap" means coming together in unison in Hindi.} approach.}~~~To address the above-mentioned issues, we formulate a problem of wireless network state prediction in the presence of dynamic interference and node mobility---more specifically, per node prediction of channel occupancy (CO) state. For each node, the prediction is used to adapt its channel hopping sequence when its channel occupancy state changes, by reassigning non-overlapping frequencies. We show that the prediction problem is solvable from historical data observed locally and passively in relatively broad contexts, e.g., where a channel hopping schedule per node is used for relatively-long lived flow traffics. Our \MILAAP solution thus introduces zero
%Note that the prediction problem is solvable in the context where the channel activities and mobility patterns are predictable on the basis of historical data. In addition, the problem can be solved in a local manner in which each node passively observes the channel occupancy states in the neighborhood. Such local prediction introduces zero 
communication overhead for prediction.

% spatial, temporal, and spectral dependencies
%In terms of prediction of channel activities in dynamic networks using the channel hopping mechanism, 
The key objective in the \MILAAP approach is to
%it is important for a prediction model to 
rapidly capture temporal, spectral, and spatial dependencies between mobile nodes. 
The channel occupancy state at a node results from the superposition of the channel hopping sequences of all transmitters within the interference range of the node. Its prediction therefore involves capturing temporospectral correlations. Moreover, since it changes as other nodes move in and out of the interference region of the node, predicting node mobility also to expedite its prediction is useful and involves capturing temporospatial correlations. \MILAAP accordingly solves the prediction problem in two phases, as depicted in Fig.~\ref{MILAAP}: the first phase addresses the channel occupancy prediction problem by capturing the regular period of the superposed channel hopping sequences. The second phase addresses the mobility detection problem by predicting the received power level of neighboring nodes to infer whether nodes are moving out from the interference region of the node. The multi-head attention mechanism has been shown to have the ability to learn complex temporospectral and temporospatial dependencies \cite{lee2019spectro, ma2019attention}. \MILAAP therefore adopts an attention-based seq2seq learning architecture \cite{cao2022spatio, yang2024multistep}.
%Consequently, our objective is to investigate the performance of attention-based solutions to capture the patterns of channel hopping sequences and mobility models.

%We propose a framework to support dynamic channel hopping with zero control overhead by locally predicting the channel occupancy map within the interference region of each node. For the prediction problem, an attention-based solution is proposed to capture the spatial, temporal, and spectral dependencies between mobile nodes. 

% \textbf{The \MILAAP approach.}~~~We design an attention-based framework to capture the spatial, temporal, and spectral dependencies between nodes in dynamic networks using frequency hopping and predict the channel occupancy map within the interference region of each node. To that end, we introduce the problem of link prediction. Then, we solve the prediction problem in two parts: solving first the channel occupancy prediction problem by capturing the regular period of the channel hopping sequences and solving thereafter the mobility detection problem by predicting the received power level of neighboring nodes to infer whether nodes are moving out from or coming into the interference region of the node. 

\vspace*{1mm}
\textbf{Contributions of the paper.} We highlight three aspects of \MILAAP: 
\vspace*{-2mm}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item 
    Its ML-based approach predicts channel occupancy states at each node using only locally and passively observed information. The prediction suffices for minimizing interference in the dynamic setting by locally adapting and scheduling channel hopping sequences. As no communication overhead is introduced in this process, it yields higher throughput efficiency for medium access scheduling as compared to MAC protocols relying on alternative proactive or reactive approaches for selecting unoccupied channels.
    \item 
    A single layer sequence to sequence masked self-attention network suffices for the first phase of \MILAAP to capture the temporospectral pattern in the superposed channel hopping sequences. When evaluated for static wireless networks, the
    pre-trained model achieves ${\sim}$100\% accuracy and also generalizes to networks where different nodes can use channel hopping sequences of different periods. 
    \item 
    Adding a multi-head masked self-attention layer for the second phase of \MILAAP suffices to capture temporospatial dependencies for predicting the trajectory of mobile nodes. The resulting pre-trained model allows \MILAAP to locally detect when nodes leave or enter the interference region of a node.  This significantly expedites the responsiveness of the node's update of its channel hopping sequence.  When evaluated in mobile wireless networks, \MILAAP 
    achieves ${\sim}$100\% accuracy again 
    in its prediction of channel occupancy.  We also show that learning is feasible for different node mobility patterns.
\end{enumerate}

