\subsection{Interference Minimization}

To characterize the interference that results from a node transmission as well as the efficiency of a channel hopping scheduler, we define a channel efficiency measure. Let $p_{i, ch, t}$ be the probability that node $i$ transmits a packet over channel $ch$ in time slot $t$ and $\Phi(i)$ denote the set of interfering nodes within node $i$'s interference region. 
%Note that other nodes in the same node as $i$ are among the sources that are within its interference region. 
Given the sender $i$ and its interference set $\Phi(i)$, the channel efficiency of channel $ch$ within $\Phi(i)$ in the time slot $t$ is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
e_{\Phi(i), ch, t} = \sum_{j\in \Phi(i)}{p_{j, ch, t} \prod_{k\in \Phi(i) \setminus \{j\}}{(1-p_{k, ch, t})}}
\label{CH_UTIL}
\end{array}
\end{equation}

\vspace*{3mm}
Let $f(i, t)$ be a channel hopping scheduling function that specifies the channel used on node $i$ at slot $t$. Consequently, the average scheduling efficiency, $\hat{E}$, can be calculated as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\hat{E}(f)= \frac{1}{\mathbb{T}} \sum_{t=0}^{\mathbb{T}-1} {\sum_{i \in V}{e_{\Phi(i), f(i, t), t}}}
\label{AVG_UTIL}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb{T}$ is the total number of slots, and $V$ is the set of nodes in the network. The channel scheduling problem is to determine a scheduler function $f$, such that $\hat{E}$ is maximized to minimize the interference.
