\subsection{Second-Phase Prediction of Received Power Levels}

%To predict the trajectory (in terms of received power level) of a node, the spatio-temporal correlation in the input should be modeled to infer the future movement. 
%Given a set of sampled channels monitored at node $i$, $K_{i}$, 

Let $RP_{t}(i) \in \mathbb{R}^{|U|}$ denote the vector of received power level of all channels observed by node $i$ at time slot $t$. In addition to the $CO_{t-\ell+1}(i), ..., CO_{t}(i)$ vectors monitored from $t-\ell+1$ to $t$, node $i$ also collects $RP_{t-\ell+1}(i), ..., RP_{t}(i)$. 
%Note that the received power level of an active node $j$ can be measured at $i$ if the distance between $i$ and $j$ is not greater than the sensing radius $r_{S}$. 
Since the ID of node $j$ transmitting over a monitored channel $ch$ at time $t$ can be unknown to node $i$, it suffices that $i$  know that $\{RP_{t-kL}(ch, i) | k = 0 ... \lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor-1 \}$ can be associated with the same node using the $L$ calculated in the first-phase prediction.\footnote{For the case where two nodes $j_1$ and $j_2$ use the same channel for communication at $t$, we assume the one with less distance to node $i$ dominates the received power level monitored at $i$.} Therefore, $\{RP_{t-kL}(ch, i) | k = 0 ... \lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor-1 \}$ can be used as the historical data sequence associated with some nodes to predict its future received power levels.
%We adopt a standard multi-head self-attention mechanism to perform the prediction of received power level. 
$SSAN_{2}$ adopts a multi-head masking self-attention layer, in which each independent head has the opportunity to learn a different mobility pattern (e.g., a node coming into or vacating $i$'s interference region). The input to $SSAN_{2}$, $X$, is $\left[RP_{t-(\lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor-1)L}(ch, i), RP_{t-(\lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor-2)L}(ch, i), ..., RP_{t}(ch, i)\right] \in \mathbb{R}^{\lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor}$.
%with $\lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor$ elements. 
$SSAN_{2}$ computes the queries ($M_{h}^{Q}$), keys ($M_{h}^{K}$), and values ($M_{h}^{V}$) for head $h$ according to Eq.~\ref{SSAN_1_kqv}. The calculation scheme in $SSAN_{2}$ is shown as follows:

\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
scores_{h} := AttentionScore(M_{h}^{Q} , M_{h}^{k}) \\
M_{h}^{W} := So\!f\!tmax(scores_{h}) \\
M_{O}:= Concat(M_{1}^{W}M_{1}^{V}, ..., M_{n}^{W}M_{n}^{V}) \\
Y := BinOutput(M_{O}W_{O}).
\label{SSAN_2}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
Each head separately calculates the attention scores and Softmax is used to normalize the scores to get the attention weight matrix $M_{h}^{W}$. The results of all the heads are concatenated into a matrix $M_{O}$ and then output as the bit matrix $Y$, denoting the prediction result. 

%The rate at which $i$ observes its channel to collect $RP$ vectors is outside the scope of our discussion. However, we note that the selection of the output length of the prediction reflects a tradeoff between the idle time between successive collections of $RP$ vectors and the prediction accuracy. Since a pre-trained model can be directly used for predicting $RP_{t+L}$ without additional fine-tuning, the multi-head attention layer is set to predict only one element to optimize the prediction results. Namely, we use the input $X$
%= [RP_{t-(\lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor-1)L}(ch, i), RP_{t-(\lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor-2)L}(ch, i), ..., RP_{t}(ch, i)]$ 
%with $\lfloor{\frac{\ell}{L}}\rfloor$ elements to predict the next received power level, $RP_{t+L}(ch, i)$.
%\linebreak 

\vspace*{1mm}
\textbf{Correction of $CO$ states.} The output $RP_{t+L}(ch, i)$ is used to correct the channel occupancy state $CO_{t+L}(ch, i)$ predicted in the first-phase.  $RP_{t+L}(ch, i) < \theta_{i}$ and $CO_{t+L}(ch, i) = 1$ imply that even though a node has moved out of the interference region of $i$ channel $ch$ is erroneously considered to interfere at $i$. In that case, $CO_{t+L}(ch, i)$ is corrected to 0. Conversely, $RP_{t+L}(ch, i) \geq \theta_{i}$ and $CO_{t+L}(ch, i) = 0$ represent that a node has entered the interference region of $i$ but is incorporated in the first-phase prediction. $CO_{t+L}(ch, i)$ is therefore corrected to 1. 
%and channel $ch$ will be considered busy at node $i$. 

