\section{Performance Evaluation}\label{evaluation}
%We demonstrate that our \MILAAP framework achieves  ${\sim}$100\% prediction accuracy of channel occupancy states in dynamic networks with different mobility models.  
%In this section, we discuss the implementation and validation of our \MILAAP framework. 
For evaluation, we implemented the \MILAAP framework using PyTorch 3.11.11 on the CUDA 12.4 compute platform. We evaluated the accuracy of 
%the two 
%masked self-attention neural networks
%sequence-to-sequence self-attention networks, 
$SSAN_{1}$ and $SSAN_{2}$, individually ---in respectively predicting the future channel occupancy map and the future received power level--- and also in an integrated fashion.  The latter demonstrated the improvement of the prediction accuracy of channel occupancy map once corrected via node mobility detection. Our evaluation experiments also assessed the generalizability of the framework, for different channel hopping sequence periods $L$.
%and different mobility models. 

\subsection{Experimental Settings}
We simulated networks with nodes distributed over a rectangular region using a uniform random distribution placement model. 
%Each node is equipped with four radios for allowing intra-node and inter-node concurrent communication. 
To simulate concurrent communication that affects the channel occupancy states for a node's interference region, all networks initiate 10 random point-to-point flows in which all routes follow the shortest path between the source and destination. Each communication node independently chooses a random channel hopping sequence with period $L$. %Table~\ref{SimulationParams} shows our simulation parameters for training and testing our framework.

To evaluate \MILAAP for different types of node mobility in the networks, we simulated three mobility models:
\vspace*{0.5mm}
\begin{itemize}
    \item 
    {\bf Fixed Mobility (FM)}: Each node selects a fixed velocity in $[0, 10]$ m/s and direction in $[0, 2\pi]$.
    \item 
    {\bf Random Waypoint (RWP)}: Each node initially chooses a random location in a random direction $a_{k} \in [0, 2\pi]$ in the simulation area as the destination $D_{k}$, and then it moves towards the destination with a random velocity in $[0, 10]$ m/s. The node then assigns a new random destination $D_{k+1}$ and a random velocity when it arrives at $D_{k}$. This is repeated until the end of the simulation.
    \item
    {\bf Smooth Random Waypoint (SRWP)}: The behavior is similar to RWP, but the assignment of the next destination $D_{k+1}$ and velocity $v_{k+1}$ depends on $a_{k}$ and $v_{k}$. A ratio $\epsilon$ is added to further limit the minimum and maximum values in the random assignment. $D_{k+1}$ is chosen to be a location in direction $a_{k+1} \in [a_{k+1}-2\pi\epsilon, a_{k+1}+2\pi\epsilon]$ and velocity $v_{k+1} \in [max(0, (1-\epsilon)v_{k+1}), min(10, (1+\epsilon)v_{k+1})]$. 
\end{itemize}
We considered both regions with and without boundaries. In the former, mobile nodes stopped when they reach any boundary location.

We specify, for the separate phase evaluation experiments, the architecture for $SSAN_1$ and $SSAN_2$ in Tables~\ref{SimulationParams_SSAN_1} and \ref{SimulationParams_SSAN_2}, respectively, and for the integrated evaluation of \MILAAP in Table~\ref{SimulationParams_IT}.

