\subsection{Simulation Setup}
\subsubsection{Inferences} 
The original USNET, consisting of $N$ UAVs, was randomly distributed within a $D\times D$ square area, where the node density is set to $\rho=200\,\text {node}/km^2$. Based on the analysis in \cite{mgc}, we set the communication distance as $d_{tr}=120\,m$. The maximum value of UAV velocity was defined as $v_{max}=10\,m/s$ for ease of calculation, and each recovery step had a time interval $\Delta t=0.1{\rm s/step}$. The maximum recovery time is set to $T_{rc}^{max}={D}/{2v_{max}}$. The number of destroyed UAVs is given by $N_D=pN$ according to the damage ratio $p$.
For the hyperparameter in the proposed algorithm, the number of residual blocks in the DGCN is $L=3$, and the hidden dimension is $d_s=512$. The learning rate is set to 0.0001 with an Adam optimizer, and the dropout ratio is set to 0.1 to alleviate overfitting.
The pre-trained model is determined as the parameters after 500 iterations under a random damage case for each $N$ with $p=0.5$.

% \subsubsection{GCN training}

\subsubsection{Metrics}
Three metrics for performance evaluation were presented in this paper. The \textit{convergent ratio} $R_c$ represents the proportion of the number of experiments that complete connectivity recovery within $T_{rc}^{max}$ to the total number of experiments. The higher $R_c$ indicates a more robust adaptability for different scenarios.
The \textit{recovery time} $T_{rc}$ is the average time for finishing the recovery process to evaluate the performance of different resilient algorithms. 
Moreover, we introduce \textit{degree distribution} $P_d$ to measure the topology uniformity of the final recovered $\mathcal{\hat{G}}_R$, which denotes the proportion of remaining nodes with a degree number not greater than $d$ versus the total number of nodes in $\mathcal{\hat{G}}_R$. A more uniform degree distribution leads to a network that is more robust against attacks \cite{distribution}.
% Obviously, a resilient network requires distribution with more low-degree nodes for better anti-destruction ability. 

