\textbf{Redundancy:} Redundancy adds to complexity, as they can obscure the unique behaviors of the model. Techniques like symmetry reduction are used to minimize this redundancy and simplify analysis \cite{emerson1996symmetry}. As discussed in section \ref{subsection: graph_litrev}, we here use Jaccard index to find similarity between two states \cite{costa2021further}. For two nodes \(u\) and \(v\) from LTS, the Jaccard Index \(J(u,v)\) is defined as:
\[
J(u, v) = \frac{|N(u) \cap N(v)|}{|N(u) \cup N(v)|}
\]
Where \(N(u)\) represents the set of neighbors of node \(u\), and  \(N(v)\) represents the set of neighbors of node \(v\).
%For symmetry, we consider Topological Symmetry - similar connectivity patterns among nodes, even if the nodes themselves are distinct. The spectrum (eigenvalues) of the adjacency matrix can be used to estimate topological symmetry. We can first compute the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix and if the graph has many repeated eigenvalues, it may indicate high symmetry \cite{chung1997spectral}.

