\section{Alternative Proof \dani{remove section}}

The following lemma is an analogue of the main lemma, Lemma~\ref{lemma:long-shortcuts-shrinks}. Its proof might shed a different light on the correctness.

\begin{lemma}
    Let $P$ be a monotone path in $G^M$ and let $F_1,\ldots, F_t$ be its funnel decomposition. Let $M'$ be the shortcuts table returned from $\LS(M)$. If $|P| \ge n^\alpha \cdot t$, then the expected length of the shortest path $P'$ in $G^{M'}$ that satisfies $g^{G^{M'}}(P')\ge g^{G^M}(P)$ is $|P'| \le c\cdot |P|$, for some constant $0<c<1$. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Let $\ell = |P|$. Consider $F_1,\ldots F_t$. We distinguish between funnels that are bounded by their first arc to those bounded by their last arc. Assume that most of the arcs of $P$ belong to funnels that are bounded by their first arc. The analysis for the other case is going to be symmetric. Among these funnels (first  bounded), we consider only funnels of length at least $n^\alpha/4$. Note that these funnels contain at least $\ell/4$ arcs. Finally, from each such funnel we take only half of the arcs, those of maximum absolute value gain (i.e., the first arcs of the funnel), \emph{except} the first arc of the funnel\footnote{This is a small technicality that will preserve laminarity of the monotone paths we find for each arc}. Denote these arcs by $e_{i_1},\ldots e_{i_r}$. Note that $r \ge \frac{\ell}{10}$.

    By Lemma~\ref{lemma:laminar}, the set $A=\{(i_j, \bar{s}(i_j)) \mid 1\le j \le r \}$ is laminar. For every $j=1,\ldots, r$, denote by~$P_j$ the monotone path that corresponds by Lemma~\ref{lemma:-bounded-to-monotone} to $(i_j, \bar{s}(i_j))$.  By Lemma~\ref{lemma:-bounded-to-monotone}, these monotone paths are also laminar.

    Consider $A' = A \cap \{ (i_j, \bar{s}(i_j)) \mid \bar{s}(i_j)-i_j \ge \bar{f}(i_j)\cdot n^\alpha/20\}$. For every $(i_j, \bar{s}(i_j))\in A'$, denote by $size'(i_j)$ the number of intervals in $A'$ which are contained in $(i_j, \bar{s}(i_j))$ (i.e., if looking at $A'$ as a tree, this is exactly the subtree size). We prove by induction on $size'(i_j)$, that with expectation, we can shortcut $P_j$ by at least $size'(i_j)/10$ arcs. Base case, $size'(i_j)=1$. Since $P$ is monotone, it follows that $\bar{f}(i_j)> 0 $ and therefore $\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j \ge \bar{f}(i_j)\cdot n^\alpha/20 \ge n^\alpha/20$. By the sampling of $\LS(M)$, $e_{i_j}$ is sampled to $T_{\lceil \log {\bar{f}(i_j)} \rceil}$  with probability $p^j = \Theta\left(\frac{\log^2 n}{n^\alpha \cdot \bar{f}(i_j)}\right)$ and by Corollary~\ref{corollary:long-shortcuts}, $P_{j}$ will be entirely shortcutted. Thus, the expected shortcutting of $P_j$ is at least $\Theta \left( \frac{\log^2 n}{n^\alpha \cdot \bar{f}(i_j)} \cdot (\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j) \right) = \Omega(\log^2 n) \ge 1/10 = size'(i_j)/10$. For the inductive step, let $i_j$ be such that $size'(i_j)>1$ and let $i_{j_1},\ldots, i_{j_x}$ be its children (when the laminar set $A'$ is viewed as a tree). Either $e_{i_j}$ is sampled to $T_{\lceil \log {\bar{f}(i_j)} \rceil}$, in which case $P_j$ is fully shortcutted, or we need to rely on shortcutting of $P_{j_1},\ldots, P_{j_x}$. Define $p^j$ as before. By the inductive hypothesis, the expected shortcutting of $P_j$ is at least

    \begin{align*}
        &p^j \cdot (\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j) + (1- p^j) \sum_{y=1}^{x}\frac{size'(i_{j_y})}{10} 
        = p^j \cdot  (\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j) +  (1- p^j) \frac{size'(i_j)-1}{10}\\
        &\ge  \frac{1}{10} p^j \cdot  (\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j) + 
         \frac{9}{10} p^j \cdot  (\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j) + (1-p^j)\frac{size'(i_j)}{10} - \frac{1}{10} \\
         &\ge \frac{1}{10} p^j \cdot  size'(i_j) + 
         \frac{9}{10} \Omega(\log^2 n) + (1-p^j)\frac{size'(i_j)}{10} - \frac{1}{10} \ge \frac{size'(i_j)}{10}
    \end{align*}

    where the last inequality follows since the interval $(i_j , \bar{s}(i_j))$ contains at most $\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j$ intervals of $A$ (and in particular of $A'$), so $\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j \ge size'(i_j)$.

    Finally, let $(i_{j_1},\bar{s}(i_{j_1})),\ldots, (i_{j_x},\bar{s}(i_{j_x})) \in A'$ be maximal with respect to inclusion (i.e., roots when $A'$ is viewed as a forest). We claim that $|A'|=\sum_{p=1}^{x} size'(i_{j_p}) = \Omega(\ell)$. This follows since the number of intervals contained in a pruned interval $(i_j, \bar{s}(i_j))$ is at most $\bar{s}(i_j)-i_j \le \bar{f}(i_j)\cdot n^\alpha/20$ and therefore, the total number of pruned intervals is at most $t \cdot n^\alpha/20 \le \ell/20$. Since $|A|\ge \ell/10$, it follows that $|A'| \ge \ell/20$. 

    Thus, the expected shortcutting of $P$ is at least $\sum_{p=1}^{x} size'(i_{j_p})/10 = |A'|/10 \ge  \ell/200$.
\end{proof}

