\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:Introduction}

Nonograms, also known as %e.g.
{\em Japanese puzzles}, {\em paint-by-numbers}, or {\em griddlers},
are a popular puzzle type where one is given an empty grid in which some
 grid cells are to be colored (filled);
%and a set of \emph {descriptions}, which constrain the set of grid cells are to be colored (filled);
the remaining cells remain empty (unfilled).
%The correct solution is derived from a set of descriptions.
For every row and column,
there is a \emph{description} that
constrains the set of colored grid cells in this row or column.
The description specifies how many consecutive blocks of cells should be filled and how large these blocks are.
Two filled blocks need to be separated by one or more unfilled cells.
% A description consists of a sequence of numbers specifying the numbers of consecutive filled cells in a row or column.
%It is usual to require %???
%that a nonogram has a unique solution; % GR: duplicated below.
A solved nonogram typically results in a picture (see Figure~\ref {fig:nonograms}).
% Moreover
% We will assume in this paper (if not stated otherwise) that
% every nonogram has exactly one solution.
%(i.e., we do not consider nonograms which do not have a solution).

Nonograms provide an accessible and contained environment for logical deduction.
They have been used successfully to teach logical thinking~\cite {10.1145/1141904.1141906,10.1007/978-3-319-94619-1_38}, and have been shown to stimulate brain activity to prevent dementia~\cite {kasinathan2020logical}.

Batenburg~\etal~\cite{Batenburg09} introduce the notion of a {\em simple} nonogram, which can be solved efficiently.
A nonogram is {\em simple} when it can be solved by only looking at a single row or column at a time.
More precisely, they consider a nonogram simple if it can be solved by repeatedly considering a row or column, enumerating all possible solutions for it
that are consistent with the fixed cells determined so far,
and fixing all cells which have the same value in any possible solution.
This procedure is called \emph{settling a row/column} (or simply \settle) and will be considered in more detail in the preliminaries (Section~\ref{sec:prelims}).
Note that since repeated application of settling a row or column is a deterministic process,
the existence of multiple solutions for a nonogram immediately implies that it cannot be simple; however, the converse is not true, i.e., there are uniquely solvable nonograms
that are not simple.
In fact, Batenburg and Kosters introduce a 
whole
hierarchy of complexity for nonograms, depending on the number of rows and columns which have to be considered simultaneously (by a specific solver)
in order to definitively identify a cell whose status
can be settled;
%has the same value in all solutions consistent with the descriptions of all considered rows and columns;
puzzles with unique solutions can be found at all levels of this hierarchy.

