	\section {Conclusions}
	\label{sec:conclusion}

	We have shown that the concept of {\em simple} nonograms extends to curved nonograms to some extent.
	In general, simple curved nonograms are not necessarily easy to solve: even the problem of testing for progress on a single description is already as hard as solving a classic nonogram under the assumption that it %this nonogram 
    has a unique solution \change{and while we suspect this problem to be \NP-hard, the complexity of solving a simple curved expert nonogram remains an open question.}
    % \textsf{NP}-hard.
	However, for the restricted classes of {\em basic} and {\em advanced} curved nonograms, we show that simple puzzles can be solved in polynomial time.
    It would be of interest how other measures of difficulty like the ones proposed by Batenburg and Kosters~\cite{DBLP:journals/icga/BatenburgK12} extend to curved nonograms.

