\section{Problem statements (removed for space)}
    When considering curved nonograms a number of natural questions arise.
    However due to the nature of puzzles, some of these problems take the form of function or promise problems rather than the classicly considered decision problem.
    In this section we give a brief overview of relevant problems.
    The following five problems are relevant to consider.\alexandra{While the enumeration here is nice, I think it takes a lot of space to put it like this for the grids and I much of it has been mentioned before, maybe it should be a bit more compact for conference?}\soeren{agreed, This should be shorter.}

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Given a classic nonogram, decide if it is solvable. This has been proven \textsf{NP}-complete by Uada and Nagao~\cite{ueda96}.
        \item Given a classic nonogram compute its solution. This is at least \textsf{NP}-hard by extension of the previous problem.
        \item Given a classic nonogram, decide if it is simple. Batenburg and Kosters dynamic program~\cite{BATENBURG20091672} can be used in repeated applications to decide this in polynmial time.
        \item Given a simple classic nonogram, compute its solution. This is similarily solved by their dynamic problem.
        \item Given a classic nonogram decide if its solution is unique. This has been proven \textsf{NP}-complete by Uada and Nagao~\cite{ueda96}.
        \item Given a classic nonogram with the guarantee that it has a unique solution, compute its solution. While there are non-simple nonograms with unique solutions, the complexity of this problem is, to best of our knowledge, so far \change{unsettled}. 
    \end{enumerate} 

    Of course we can ask a similar question about curved nonograms.
    Note that since curved nonograms are a generalization of classic nonograms, hardness results immediately translate to this setting and are not listed again.

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Given a curved nonogram, decide if it is simple. We show in this paper that this can be decided in polynomial time for basic and advanced nonograms.
        \item Given a curved nonogram, compute its solution. As for classic nonograms, our results for the previous problem can be used to solve this problem in polynomial time for basic and advanced nonograms.
    \end{enumerate}


