	\section {Solving Simple Basic Curved Nonograms}
    \label{sec:basic}

    We start by recalling the algorithm by Batenburg and Kosters~\cite{BATENBURG20091672}, which applies almost directly to the case of basic curved nonograms.
    They present a dynamic program, which, given a specification $\Psi$ and a description, decides if there is a fix that refines , which solves a generalized problem.
    Specifically it is capable of handling descriptions, which allow for any number of different symbols in a solution (i.e., they consider any alphabet $\Sigma$) and any of these characters can be required to appear in blocks with a minimal and a maximal length.
    For a given specification $\Psi$ and a decription $D$, the function $\fix(\Psi, D)$ if true if there is a fix of $\Psi$, which is consistent with $D$.
    Their dynamic program computes a table of values of this function $\fix (\Psi[i],D[j])$ (of simply $\fix (i,j)$ as a shorthand), for increasing $i$ and $j$.

    They do so using the following recursion

	\[
	\fix (i, j) = \bigvee_{p = \max (i-b_j, A_{j-1}, L_i^{\sigma_j}(s))}^{\min(i-a_j, B_{j-1})} \fix (p, j-1)
	\]

    Here 

    In a nonogram we only consider the symbols $\desc{0}$ and $\desc{1}$ and for any block of $\desc{1}$s, the size of that block is fixed, while for $\desc{0}$s, the minimal length is 0 (if its a leading or trailing block of the sequence) or 1 (if there is a block of $\desc{1}$s before and after it) and the maximal length can be considered unbounded or bounded to at most the length of the entire sequence.
    We can therefore simplify the recursion to the following.

    \[
	\fix (i, j) = \bigvee_{p = \max (i-a_j, A_{j-1}, L_i^{\sigma_j}(s))}^{i-a_j} \fix (p, j-1)
	\]


	\begin {theorem}\label{thm:basic}
	In a simple basic curved nonograms we can make progress on a specification in polynomial time.
	\end {theorem}
    \begin{proof}




    \end{proof}

    \begin{corollary}
        Any simple basic curved nonogram can be solved in polynomial time.
    \end{corollary}

