\section{The Quasirandom Group Case}\label{sec:quasirandom}

In this section, we provide a full proof of the following generalized version of \Cref{thm:mainSL}. 
Note that several parts of the presentation follow almost verbatim from their analogs in \Cref{sec:finite-field}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:main_full_qr}
    There exists a constant $c > 0$ such that the following holds. Let $G$ be a $Q$-quasirandom group (as in \Cref{def:quasirandom_grp}) and $A \subseteq G \times G$ with no $x,y,g \in G$ with $g \neq \mathbf{1}_G$ such that $(x,y), (xg,y), (x,gy) \in A$. Then
    $$
        |A| \leq |G|^2 \cdot \exp(-c(\log Q)^{1/11}).
    $$
\end{theorem}

Before proceeding to the details, we review some additional background and useful results about quasirandom groups. 

\subsection{Background}
The notion of quasirandom groups was introduced by Gowers \cite{Gow08} in studying product-free sets. 
Quasirandom groups enjoy the property that for any two large sets $A, B \subseteq G$, the distribution obtained by taking uniform random samples $a \in A$, $b \in B$ and outputting $ab$ is close to uniform in an $L_2$-sense. 
Of course, abelian groups fail to satisfy the mixing property mentioned above. 
In $\mb{F}_2^n$ for instance, a subspace $V$ of codimension 1 is very dense, but $V + V = V$ is far from being uniform. 
We now present the formal definition. 
\begin{definition}[$Q$-Quasirandom group]\label{def:quasirandom_grp}
    Let $Q \ge 1$. A finite group $G$ is \emph{$Q$-quasirandom} if every nontrivial irreducible representation over $\mathbb{C}$ has dimension at least $Q$. 
\end{definition}
One can show that every irreducible representation of a finite group $G$ must have dimension at most $\sqrt{|G|}$. We record this fact for later use, which can be found in most introductory texts on representation theory of finite groups. For example, this follows from Proposition 5 in \cite[Chapter 2.4]{Ser77}.
\begin{fact}\label{fct:trivdim}
    Every complex irreducible representation of a finite group $G$ has dimension at most $\sqrt{|G|}$. 
\end{fact}
 There are choices of groups $G$ which are $Q$-quasirandom for $Q = |G|^{\Omega(1)}$. Contrast this with abelian groups, where every irreducible representation has dimension 1. A common example of a quasirandom group is 
$$
    \mathrm{SL}_2(\mb{F}_p) = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix}
        a & b \\
        c & d
    \end{pmatrix} : a,b,c,d \in \mb{F}_p, ad-bc = 1 \right\},
$$
the set of $2 \times 2$ matrices over the finite field $\mb{F}_p$ for $p$ prime with determinant 1. 
A classical result of Frobenius shows that every nontrivial irreducible representation of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mb{F}_p)$ has dimension at least $\frac{p-1}{2} \sim |G|^{1/3}$. (For exposition of these facts and additional background on quasirandom groups, see e.g.~\cite{Tao11}.) 

Quasirandom groups have seen various application in constructing pseudorandom objects. The Ramanujan graphs of Lubotzky, Phillips, and Sarnak \cite{LPS88} are built from Cayley graphs of $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mb{F}_p)$ for specific choices of $p$. (The group $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mb{F}_p)$ is obtained by quotienting $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mb{F}_p)$ by its center.) Bourgain and Gamburd \cite{BG08} showed that random Cayley graphs on $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mb{F}_p)$ are expanders. The quasirandomness property was useful for arguing about the multiplicity of eigenvalues of the Cayley graph. Outside of graph theory, ideas from the study of quasirandom groups led to an optimal inapproximability result for $k$-LIN over non-abelian groups \cite{BK21}. A recent work of Derksen, Lee, and Viola \cite{DLV24} building on \cite{GV19} proved $k$-NOF lower bounds for computing an ``interleaved product'' of elements coming from a quasirandom group. In particular, their bounds are best understood in the regime where $k$ is growing, and they match that of \cite{BNS89}.  

For a finite group $G$, denote the convolution of functions $f,g : G \to \mb{R}$ by $(f \ast g)(x) = \E_{y \in G}f(y)g(y^{-1}x)$. The most useful property of quasirandom groups is that convolutions of dense sets mix. More formally, we have the following: 
\begin{theorem}[\cite{Gow08, BNP08}]\label{thm:convolution_mixing_quasi}
    Let $G$ be a $Q$-quasirandom group and $f, g : G \to \mathbb{R}$, and assume at least one has mean zero. Then
    $$
        \| f \ast g\|_{2} \leq Q^{-1/2} \|f\|_{2} \|g\|_{2}.
    $$
\end{theorem}
There are various proofs of this fact \cite{BNP08, Gow08, Tao11} which use representation theory. Over abelian groups, convolutions and $L_2$-norms have pleasant interpretations when working in the Fourier basis. Over nonabelian groups, a similar theory exists which can be formulated using representation theory. 

\Cref{thm:convolution_mixing_quasi} can be used to count solutions to equations in quasirandom groups. \cite{Gow08} did exactly this to bound the size of the largest product-free set in a quasirandom group. Namely, we can count solutions $(x,y)$ where $x \in X, y \in Y, xy \in Z$. We have the following:
\begin{corollary}[\cite{Gow08, BNP08}]\label{cor:quasirandomness_mixing}
    Let $G$ be a $Q$-quasirandom group, and let $f,g,h : G \to [-1,1]$. Then, 
    $$
        \left| \E_{x,y \in G} [f(x) g(y) h(xy)] - \E[f]\E[g]\E[h] \right| \leq Q^{-1/2}\|f\|_2 \|g\|_2\|h\|_2.
    $$
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
    Let $\widetilde{f} = f - \E[f]$.
    We have
    \begin{align*}
        \E_{x,y \in G} [f(x) g(y) h(xy)] 
        &= \E_{z \in G}[(f \ast g)(z) h(z)] \\
        &= \E[f]\E[g]\E[h] + \E_{z \in G}[(\widetilde{f} \ast g)(z)h(z)] \\
        &= \E[f]\E[g]\E[h] \pm \|\widetilde{f} \ast g\|_2 \|h\|_2 \tag{Cauchy-Schwarz} \\
        &= \E[f]\E[g]\E[h] \pm Q^{-1/2}\|\widetilde{f}\|_2 \|g\|_2\|h\|_2. \tag{\Cref{thm:convolution_mixing_quasi}}
    \end{align*}
    Since $\widetilde{f}$ and $\E[f]$ are orthogonal and $f$ is 1-bounded, we have $\|\widetilde{f}\|_2 = \left(\E[f^2] - \E[f]^2 \right)^{1/2} \leq \|f\|_2$. 
\end{proof}

Note that we can also use \Cref{cor:quasirandomness_mixing} to count other simple patterns, such as tuples $(x,y,x^{-1}y) \in X \times Y \times Z$ or $(x,y,xy^{-1}) \in X \times Y \times Z$.

At this point, we have the necessary tools to prove \Cref{thm:mainSL}.

\subsection{General setup}
Throughout, we will work with a $Q$-quasirandom group $G$. 
We will consider subsets $X, Y, D \subseteq G$ of densities $\delta_X, \delta_Y, \delta_D$, and work within their \emph{container set}
$$
    S(X, Y, D) \coloneqq \{ (x,y) \in G \times G \colon x \in X, y \in Y, xy \in D \} \subseteq G \times G.
$$
Notice that for dense sets $X, Y, D$, \Cref{cor:quasirandomness_mixing} guarantees that $|S(X, Y, D)| \approx \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D |G|^2$, or more precisely:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:SXYD_deviation_qr}
    \bigg||S(X, Y, D)| - \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D |G|^2\bigg| \le Q^{-1/2}|G|^2.
\end{equation}
This will be one of two main ways that quasirandomness is utilized in the proof. The other way will be to construct sets which have small \emph{cut norms}. 

\subsection{Cut norms}
Our primary measure of pseudorandomness throughout this section will be the \textit{cut norm}, sometimes referred to as discrepancy (see \cite{CFZ14}, for example). 

\begin{definition}[Cut norm \& pseudorandom]\label{def:cut-norm}
Let $\Omega_1,~\Omega_2$ be finite sets and $T:\Omega_1\times\Omega_2\to \mb{R}$ be a function. We define the \emph{cut norm} of $T$ as
\[\snorm{T}_{\Box} = \sup_{\substack{g_1:\Omega_1\to [-1,1]\\g_2:\Omega_2\to [-1,1]}}\E_{\substack{x\in \Omega_1\\y\in \Omega_2}}[g_1(x)g_2(y)T(x,y)].\]
We say $T:\Omega_1\times\Omega_2\to [0,1]$ is \emph{$\gamma$-pseudorandom} if 
\[ \|T - \E_{x\in \Omega_1, y\in \Omega_2}[T(x,y)]\|_{\Box} \le \gamma. \]
\end{definition}
Occasionally, we will refer to a set as pseudorandom if its indicator function is pseudorandom. 
Note that $\gamma$-pseudorandomness can be viewed as a \textit{two-sided} assumption on how $T$ correlates with rectangles, while \textit{combinatorial spreadness} (see \Cref{def:combpseudo}) only gives upper bounds on how $T$ correlates with rectangles. It was critical for us to work with this weaker, one-sided assumption in order to obtain quasipolynomial bounds in the abelian group setting (see the discussion in \Cref{sec:overview}). On the other hand, we can obtain stronger quantitative bounds in the quasirandom setting because we have easy access to this stronger, two-sided assumption of $\gamma$-pseudorandomness. This ends up coming into play when we prove \Cref{lem:sparsevonneumann}.

We now come to the second way in which we will utilize the quasirandomness of $G$. We will argue that for large enough sets $X, Y, D \subseteq G$, the set $T \coloneqq \{(x,y) : xy \in D \} \subseteq X \times Y$ is $\gamma$-pseudorandom.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:psd_from_qr}
    Let $G$ be a $Q$-quasirandom group, and let $X, Y, D \subseteq G$ be subsets with size $|X|, |Y| \geq |G|/Q^{1/8}$. 
    Then, the set $T \coloneqq \{(x,y) : xy \in D\} \subseteq X \times Y$ is $2Q^{-1/4}$-pseudorandom. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Let $\tau = |T|/|X||Y|$. We want to argue that
    for any functions $g_1: X \to [-1,1]$ and $g_2 : Y \to [-1,1]$, we have
    \[
        \left| \E_{\substack{x \in X \\ y \in Y}}[g_1(x)g_2(y)(\mbm{1}_T - \tau)(x,y)] \right| = \left| \E_{\substack{x \in X \\ y \in Y}}[g_1(x)g_2(y)\mbm{1}_T(x,y)] - \tau\E_{x \in X}[g_1(x)] \E_{y \in Y}[g_2(y)] \right| \leq 2Q^{-1/4}.
    \]
    First, we will argue that $\tau$ is approximately $\delta_D$. We have
    $$
        |\tau - \delta_D|= \left| \E_{\substack{x \in X \\ y \in Y}}[\mbm{1}_T(x,y) - \delta_D] \right| = (\delta_X \delta_Y)^{-1} \left|\E_{x,y \in G}[\mbm{1}_X(x)\mbm{1}_{Y}(y)(\mbm{1}_D - \delta_D)(xy)] \right| \leq (\delta_X\delta_Y)^{-1}Q^{-1/2}
    $$
    by \Cref{cor:quasirandomness_mixing}. Recalling that $\mbm{1}_{T}(x,y) = \mbm{1}_D(xy)$, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \left| \E_{\substack{x \in X \\ y \in Y}}[g_1(x)g_2(y)\mbm{1}_T(x,y)] - \tau\E_{x \in X}[g_1(x)] \E_{y \in Y}[g_2(y)] \right| &\le |\tau - \delta_D| + \left| \E_{\substack{x \in X \\ y \in Y}}[g_1(x)g_2(y)(\mbm{1}_T - \delta_D)(x,y)] \right| \\
        & \leq |\tau - \delta_D| \\
        &\quad + (\delta_X \delta_Y)^{-1} \left| \E_{x,y \in G}[g_1(x)g_2(y)(\mbm{1}_D - \delta_D)(xy)] \right| \\
        &\leq 2(\delta_X \delta_Y)^{-1} Q^{-1/2} \\
        &\leq 2Q^{-1/4},
    \end{align*}
    where the third inequality follows from \Cref{cor:quasirandomness_mixing}, and in the last inequality we used the fact that $\delta_X, \delta_Y \geq Q^{-1/8}$. 
\end{proof}
We note that the same conclusion holds for the sets $\{(x,y) : x^{-1}y \in D\}$ and $\{(x,y) : xy^{-1} \in D\}$. 

\subsection{Von Neumann lemma}

In this subsection, we give suitable conditions under which a set $A \subseteq S(X,Y,Z)$ contains roughly as many corners as a random set of the same density. More specifically, for a set $X \subseteq G$, let $\delta_X = |X|/|G|$. Suppose $A \subseteq S(X,Y,D)$ has size $|A| = \alpha \delta_X\delta_Y\delta_D|G|^2$. For sets $X, Y, D$ which are dense enough, the size of $S(X,Y,D)$ will be roughly $\delta_X\delta_Y\delta_D|G|^2$ (see \cref{eq:SXYD_deviation_qr}), so one should morally view $\alpha$ as the density of $A$ within its container $S(X, Y, D)$.

Define the trilinear form $\Phi(f_1, f_2, f_3) \coloneqq \E_{x,y,z \in G}\left[f_1(x, y)f_2(zy^{-1}, y)f_3(x, x^{-1}z) \right]$. 
Observe that by the change of variable $z \to xgy$, $\Phi(f_1, f_2, f_3) = \E_{x,y,g \in G}\left[f_1(x, y)f_2(xg, y)f_3(x, gy) \right]$, so that $\Phi(\mbm{1}_A,\mbm{1}_A,\mbm{1}_A)$ counts the number of corners in $A$ (up to normalization). 
We will show that if $A$ is sufficiently pseudorandom, then $\Phi(\mbm{1}_A,\mbm{1}_A,\mbm{1}_A)$ is at least a constant factor times $\alpha^3 \delta_X^2 \delta_Y^2 \delta_D^2$, which is roughly the number of corners one would expect had $A$ been chosen randomly from $S(X, Y, D)$. Concretely, for sufficiently quasirandom $G$, we will require two grid norms related to $\mbm{1}_A$ to be bounded, as well as require that $A$ has no columns which are too sparse.

We stress that the main difference between \Cref{lem:sparsevonneumann} and \Cref{lem:VNL} is the choice of $p$. Here, the choice of $p$ depends only on $\alpha$ since we have easy access to $\gamma$-pseudorandomness. In \Cref{lem:VNL}, the choice of $p$ depends on $\alpha$ as well as $\delta_D$, which is one reason we can obtain stronger quantitative bounds in the quasirandom setting. This is also why we required stronger lower bounds on $\delta_D$ when working in the abelian setting. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sparsevonneumann} 
    Let $G$ be a $Q$-quasirandom group, and let $X, Y, D \subseteq G$ be subsets of size $|X|=\delta_X |G|, |Y|=\delta_Y |G|, |D|=\delta_D |G|$.
    Additionally, let $A \subseteq S(X, Y, D)$ be a subset of size $|A| = \alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D |G|^2$. Let $\eps \in (0,1/10)$ and $p = \Omega(\log(1/\alpha) / \eps^4)$ be a positive integer. Define the functions $F_1 : Y \times D \to \{0, 1\}$ where $F_1(y, z) = \mbm{1}_A(zy^{-1}, y)$ and $F_2 : X \times D \to \{0, 1\}$ where $F_2(x, z) = \mbm{1}_A(x, x^{-1}z)$. Suppose the following conditions hold: 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\snorm{F_1}_{G(2,p)} < (1+\eps^2/36)\alpha \delta_X$,
        \item $\snorm{F_2}_{G(2,p)} < 2\alpha \delta_Y$,
        \item For all $y \in Y$, we have $\E_{x \in G}[\mbm{1}_A(x,y)] \geq (1-\eps^2/36)\alpha \delta_X \delta_D$,
        \item $Q^{-1} \le (\eps \alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D)^{O(p)}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then,
    $$
        \Phi(\mbm{1}_A,\mbm{1}_A,\mbm{1}_A) \geq (1 - 12\eps)\alpha^3 \delta_X^2 \delta_Y^2 \delta_D^2.
    $$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    For clarity, let $f \coloneqq \mbm{1}_A$ denote the indicator function for $A$. Additionally, let $S \coloneqq S(G, Y, D) = \{(zy^{-1}, y) \in G \times G : y\in Y, z\in D\}$, and let $h = f - \alpha \delta_X \mbm{1}_S$. Then, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \Phi(f,f,f) = \Phi(f,h,f) + \alpha \delta_X \cdot \Phi(f, \mbm{1}_S, f).
    \end{align*}
    We will proceed by lower bounding the second term. Afterwards, we will upper bound the magnitude of the first term, showing it is ultimately dominated by the second.

    Using the observation that whenever $f(x,y) = 1$ and $f(x,x^{-1}z)=1$ we must have $x\in X, y\in Y, z\in D$, we can lower bound
    \begin{align*}
        \Phi(f, \mbm{1}_S, f) &= \E_{x,y,z \in G}\left[f(x, y)\mbm{1}_S(zy^{-1}, y)f(x, x^{-1}z) \right] \\
        &= \E_{x,y,z \in G}\left[f(x, y)\mbm{1}_Y(y)\mbm{1}_D(z)f(x, x^{-1}z) \right] \\
        &= \E_{x,y,z \in G}\left[f(x, y)f(x, x^{-1}z) \right] \\ 
        &= \E_{x \in G} \left(\E_{y \in G} f(x, y)\right)^2 \ge \delta_X^{-1}\left(\E_{x,y \in G} f(x, y)\right)^2 = \alpha^2\delta_X \delta_Y^2\delta_D^2,
    \end{align*}
    where we used the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the last line. In particular, 
$$
    \alpha \delta_X \cdot \Phi(f, \mbm{1}_S, f)  \geq \alpha^3 \delta_X^2 \delta_Y^2 \delta_D^2.
$$
Thus, it remains to bound the magnitude of $\Phi(f,h,f)$. 
Since $f(x,y)$ is supported on $T \coloneqq S(X,Y,D)$, it suffices to take the expectation over $(x,y) \in T$. Let $|T| = \tau|X||Y|$ and set $k = 2\lceil \log(1/\alpha) \rceil$. Then, we have
\begin{align}
    |\Phi(f,h,f)| &= \left|\E_{x,y,z \in G} f(x,y)h(zy^{-1},y)f(x,x^{-1}z)\right| \notag \\
    &= \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D \left|\E_{x \in X, y \in Y, z \in D} f(x,y)h(zy^{-1},y)f(x,x^{-1}z)\right| \notag \\
    &= \tau \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D \left|\E_{(x,y) \in T} f(x,y) \left( \E_{z \in D} h(zy^{-1},y)f(x,x^{-1}z)\right) \right| \notag \\
    &\leq \tau \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D \left( \frac{|A|}{|T|} \right)^{1-\frac{1}{k}} \left( \E_{(x,y) \in T} \left| \E_{z \in D} h(zy^{-1},y)f(x,x^{-1}z)\right|^k \right)^{1/k}, \label{eq:two_factors_qr}
\end{align}
where the last inequality follows by H\"{o}lder's inequality. 
Using \Cref{eq:SXYD_deviation_qr} and the assumption $Q^{-1} \leq (\eps\alpha \delta_X \delta_D \delta_Y)^{O(k)}$, we can show that $\tau$ is roughly $\delta_D$: 
\[
    \delta_D / 2 \leq \delta_D - (\delta_X \delta_Y)^{-1}Q^{-1/2} \leq \tau \leq \delta_D + (\delta_X \delta_Y)^{-1}Q^{-1/2} \leq 2\delta_D.
\]
Combining this bound with our choice of $k$ and our upper bound on $\tau$, we find
\[
    \tau \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D \left( \frac{|A|}{|T|} \right)^{1-\frac{1}{k}} = \tau \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D  \left( \frac{\alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D}{\tau \delta_X \delta_Y} \right)^{1-\frac{1}{k}}
    \le \tau \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D \left(\frac{\alpha \delta_D}{\tau} \right)\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}\right)^{-\frac{1}{k}} \le 4\alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D^2.
\]
Thus, it suffices to bound the final factor of \Cref{eq:two_factors_qr} by $3\eps \alpha^2 \delta_X \delta_Y$.

Define the function $H : Y \times D \to [-1, 1]$ as $H(y, z) = h(zy^{-1}, y)$, and recall that $F_2(x,z) = f(x,x^{-1}z)$. Rewriting, we have 
\begin{align*} \left( \E_{(x,y) \in T} \left| \E_{z \in D} h(zy^{-1},y)f(x,x^{-1}z)\right|^k \right)^{1/k} &= \left(\E_{(x,y) \in T} \left| \E_{z \in D} H(y,z)F_2(x,z) \right|^k \right)^{1/k} \\
&= \left(\E_{\substack{(x,y) \in T \\ z_1, \dots, z_k \in D}} \prod_{i=1}^k H(y,z_i)F_2(x,z_i) \right)^{1/k} \\
&= \left(\tau^{-1} \E_{\substack{x\in X, y\in Y \\ z_1, \dots, z_k \in D}} \left[\mbm{1}_T(x,y) \left(\prod_{i=1}^k H(y,z_i)\right) \left(\prod_{i=1}^k F_2(x,z_i) \right)\right]\right)^{1/k} \\
&\leq \left(\tau^{-1} \left(2Q^{-1/4} + \tau \E_{\substack{x \in X, y \in Y \\ z_1, \dots, z_k \in D}} \prod_{i=1}^k H(y,z_i)F_2(x,z_i) \right)\right)^{1/k}
\end{align*}
where the last inequality follows from the fact that $T$ is $2Q^{-1/4}$-pseudorandom by \Cref{lem:psd_from_qr}. 
Now, using our lower bound on $\tau$ and the assumption $Q^{-1} \leq (\eps\alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D)^{O(k)}$, we know
\[
    (2\tau^{-1}Q^{-1/4})^{1/k} \le \left(\frac{4Q^{-1/4}}{\delta_D}\right)^{1/k} \le \eps \alpha^2 \delta_X \delta_Y.
\]
Hence by concavity it suffices to show that
$$
    \left(\E_{\substack{x \in X, y \in Y \\ z_1, \dots, z_k \in D}} \prod_{i=1}^k H(y,z_i)F_2(x,z_i) \right)^{1/k} \leq 2\eps \alpha^2 \delta_X \delta_Y.
$$
By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have
\begin{align*}
    \left(\E_{\substack{x \in X, y \in Y \\ z_1, \dots, z_k \in D}} \prod_{i=1}^k H(y,z_i)F_2(x,z_i) \right)^{1/k} &\leq 
    \left(\E_{\substack{y_1,y_2 \in Y \\ z_1,\dots,z_k \in D }}\prod_{i=1}^k H(y_1, z_i)H(y_2, z_i)\right)^{1/(2k)} \\
    &\qquad \cdot \left(\E_{\substack{x_1,x_2 \in X \\ z_1,\dots,z_k \in D }} \prod_{i=1}^k F_2(x_1, z_i)F_2(x_2,z_i) \right)^{1/(2k)} \\
    &= \|H\|_{G(2,k)}\cdot \|F_2\|_{G(2,k)}.
\end{align*}
The second hypothesis, along with the fact that grid norms are monotonic, bounds the second factor by $2\alpha\delta_Y$.

We will finish the proof by bounding the first factor by $\eps \alpha \delta_X$. 
To do this, we will use the third item of the hypothesis as well as \Cref{lem:spectral-pos} to reduce this quantity to the first hypothesis. 
First, note that
\[ \|F_1\|_{G(1,1)} = (\delta_{Y}\delta_D)^{-1}\E_{y, z \in G}[f(zy^{-1},y)] = \alpha\delta_X \] by the definition of $\alpha$. Thus $H = F_1 - \|F_1\|_{G(1,1)}$ by the definition of $h$. 
Observe the third item of the hypothesis guarantees lower--boundedness on the rows of $H$; namely for all $y \in Y$, we have that
$$
    \E_{z \in D}[H(y,z)] = \delta_D^{-1} \E_{z \in G}[h(zy^{-1}, y)] \geq (1-\eps^2/36)\alpha \delta_X - \alpha \delta_X = -\eps^2 \alpha \delta_X/36. 
$$
Combining  with the first item of the hypothesis, the contrapositive of \cref{lem:spectral-pos} gives that
\[
    \|H\|_{G(2,k)} < \eps\alpha \delta_X,
\]
as desired.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Density increment}

Now we prove that if the grid norm assumptions of \Cref{lem:sparsevonneumann} are not satisfied, we can pass to a subset $X' \times Y' \times Z'$ where (essentially) the density of $A$ in $S(X', Y', Z')$ increases by a constant factor. 
Our main tools will be \cref{thm:quasisifting2} and \cref{lem:extract-cor}.
We remark that while the asymmetric conclusion present in \cref{thm:quasisifting2} is essential for our analysis over abelian groups, it is not necessary for the current setting. This is because the choice of $p$ in \Cref{lem:sparsevonneumann} depends only on $\alpha$, whereas the the choice of $p$ in \Cref{lem:VNL} depends on $\alpha$ as well as $\delta_D$. Thus, it was crucial that the density of $D'$ in the conclusion of \Cref{lemma:densincr} did not depend on $k$ (see \Cref{sec:overview_asymmetric_inc} for further discussion). 

For the sake of brevity, we will simply use combinatorial spreadness as our pseudorandomness notion in this subsection to apply \cref{thm:quasisifting2} directly.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma:quasidensincr}
Let $G$ be a $Q$-quasirandom group, and let $X, Y, D \subseteq G$ be subsets of size $|X|=\delta_X |G|, |Y|=\delta_Y |G|, |D|=\delta_D |G|$.
Additionally, let $A \subseteq S(X, Y, D)$ be a subset of size $|A| = \alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D |G|^2$. Let $\eps \in (0,1/10)$ and $k$ be a positive integer.  
Define the functions $F_1 : Y \times D \to \{0, 1\}$ where $F_1(y, z) = \mbm{1}_A(zy^{-1}, y)$ and $F_2 : X \times D \to \{0, 1\}$ where $F_2(x, z) = \mbm{1}_A(x,x^{-1}z)$. Suppose $X,Y,D$ satisfy $\delta_X, \delta_Y, \delta_D \geq Q^{-1/8}$, and 
$$
    Q^{-1} \leq (\eps\alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D)^{O(\eps^{-2}k\log(1/\alpha)^2 + \eps^{-1}k\log(1/(\delta_X \delta_Y)))}
$$

Then if $\snorm{F_1}_{G(2,k)} \ge (1+\eps)\alpha\delta_X,$ then there are $Y' \subseteq Y$ and $D' \subseteq D$ with $|Y'| \ge (\eps\alpha/2)^{O(\eps^{-1}k^2\log(1/\alpha))}|Y|$ and $|D'| \ge (\eps\alpha/2)^{O(\eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha))}|D|$, and 
\[ |A \cap S(X, Y', D')| \ge (1+\Omega(\eps))\alpha\delta_X|Y'||D'|. \]
Similarly, if $\snorm{F_2}_{G(2,k)} \ge (1+\eps)\alpha\delta_Y,$
then there are $X' \subseteq X$ and $D' \subseteq D$ with $|X'| \ge (\eps\alpha/2)^{O(\eps^{-1}k^2\log(1/\alpha))}|X|$ and $|D'| \ge (\eps \alpha/2)^{O(\eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha))}|D|$, and
\[ |A \cap S(X', Y, D')| \ge (1+\Omega(\eps))\alpha\delta_Y|X'||D'|. \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We only prove the former assertion, as the latter one has an identical proof. 
Let $\eps_s = \Theta(\eps)$ sufficiently small, and consider the set $T \coloneqq \{(y,z) \in Y \times D : zy^{-1} \in X\}$ of size $|T| = |S(X,Y,Z)| = \tau |Y||D|$.
We will use \Cref{eq:SXYD_deviation_qr} as well as our assumption $Q^{-1/2} \leq \eps_s \delta_X \delta_Y\delta_D$ to argue that $\tau$ is roughly $\delta_X$:
\[
    \tau \leq \delta_X+(\delta_Y \delta_D)^{-1}Q^{-1/2} \leq (1+\eps_s)\delta_X.
\]
The same argument also shows that $(1-\eps_s)\delta_X \leq \tau$. We can than use this to write our grid norm assumption in terms of $\tau$:
$$
    \|F_1\|_{G(2,k)} \geq \left(1+\eps\right)\alpha \delta_X \geq \left(1+\eps\right)(1+\eps_s)^{-1} \alpha\tau \ge \left(1+\frac{\eps}{2}\right)\alpha\tau.
$$
Since $Y$ and $D$ are large enough, we may apply \Cref{lem:psd_from_qr} to deduce that $T$ is $2Q^{-1/4}$-pseudorandom, and so it follows from the definition that $T$ is $(\tau, 2Q^{-1/2})$-combinatorially spread. We want to apply \Cref{thm:quasisifting2} using $T$ as a container, so it remains to verify that $T$ is sufficiently spread. In particular, we need
$$
    2Q^{-1/2} \leq (\alpha \tau)^{O(\eps^{-2} k \log(1/\alpha)^2 + \eps^{-1} k \log(1/\tau))}.
$$
Since we already showed that $\tau \leq 2\delta_X$, our assumption on $Q$ satisfies the above inequality.
Thus applying \cref{thm:quasisifting2} and \cref{lem:extract-cor} with our choice of $\eps_s = \Theta(\eps)$ sufficiently small gives that there are functions $g_1: Y \to \{0, 1\}$ and $g_2: D \to \{0, 1\}$ satisfying:
\[ \E_{y \in Y}[g_1(y)] \ge (\eps\alpha/2)^{O(\eps^{-1}k^2\log(1/\alpha))} \enspace \quad\text{and}\quad \enspace \E_{z \in D}[g_2(z)] \ge (\eps\alpha/2)^{O(\eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha))}\]
and
\begin{align*}
    \E_{y \in Y, z \in D}[F_1(y,z)g_1(y)g_2(z)] &\geq (1-\eps_s)(1+\eps/2)\alpha \tau \E_{y \in Y}[g_1(y)] \E_{z \in D}[g_2(z)] \\
    &\geq (1+\eps/4) \alpha \tau \E_{y \in Y}[g_1(y)] \E_{z \in D}[g_2(z)] \\
    &\geq (1+\eps/4)(1-\eps_s) \alpha \delta_X\E_{y \in Y}[g_1(y)] \E_{z \in D}[g_2(z)] \\
    &\geq (1+\eps/8) \alpha \delta_X\E_{y \in Y}[g_1(y)] \E_{z \in D}[g_2(z)].
\end{align*}
Letting $Y'$ and $D'$ be the indicator functions of $g_1$ and $g_2$ respectively completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Obtaining spreadness}
In this subsection, we will use a density increment algorithm to reach a state $A \subseteq S(X,Y,D)$ where the conditions of the von Neumann Lemma (\Cref{lem:sparsevonneumann}) are satisfied.
We recall the useful definition of asymmetric combinatorial spreadness (\cref{def:asymspread,def:aspread}): a function $f : \Omega_1 \times \Omega_2 \to [0,1]$ is \emph{$(s,t,\eps)$-combinatorially spread} if for for all functions $g_1 : \Omega_1 \to \{0,1\}$ and $g_2 : \Omega_2 \to \{0,1\}$ with
    $$
        \E[g_1(x)] \geq 2^{-s} \quad\text{and}\quad \E[g_2(y)] \geq 2^{-t},
    $$
    it holds that
    $$ 
        \E[f(x,y)g_1(x)g_2(y)] \leq (1+\eps)\E[f]\E[g_1]\E[g_2].
    $$
    Note that unlike \Cref{def:combpseudo}, the $g_i$ are simply subsets of $\Omega_i$, rather than $[0,1]$-valued functions.
    Moreover, we say $A$ is \emph{$(s,t,\eps)$-combinatorially spread} in a container $S(X, Y, D)$ if the functions $F_1 : Y \times D \to \{0,1\}$, $F_2 : X \times D \to \{0,1\}$ defined as
    $$
        F_1(y,z) = \mbm{1}_{A \cap S(X, Y, D)}(zy^{-1}, y) \quad\text{and}\quad F_2(x,z) = \mbm{1}_{A \cap S(X, Y, D)}(x, x^{-1}z)
    $$
    are $(s,t,\eps)$-combinatorially spread.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:obtaining_spreadness_qr}
    Let $G$ be a $Q$-quasirandom group.
    Let $t \geq 1$ and $\eps \in (0,1/64)$. Suppose that $A \subseteq G \times G$ has size $|A| = \alpha|G|^2$. As long as $s,t \leq O\left(\frac{\eps\log Q}{\log(1/\alpha)}\right)$, then there exist sets $X , Y, D \subseteq G$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The set $A$ is $(s,t,5\eps^{1/2})$-combinatorially spread in the container $S(X, Y, D)$. 
        \item $|D| \geq 2^{-O(t \eps^{-1} \log (1/\alpha)}|G|$.
        \item $|X|, |Y| \geq 2^{-O(s\eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha))}|G|$.
        \item The columns of $A \cap S(X, Y, D)$ are lower bounded; namely for all $y \in Y$, 
        $$
            \E_{x \in G}[\mbm{1}_{A \cap S(X, Y, D)}(x,y)] \geq (1-2\eps^{1/2})\alpha^* \delta_X \delta_D,
        $$
        where $\alpha^* = \frac{|A \cap S(X, Y, D)|}{\delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D |G|^2} \geq \alpha$. 
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Perform the following algorithm, beginning with $X, Y, D = G$. As long as $A$ is not $(s+1,t,\eps)$-combinatorially spread in its container $S(X, Y, D)$, iteratively restrict to containers $S(X_1, Y, D_1)$ or $S(X, Y_1, D_1)$ to obtain a $(1+\eps)$ density increment as is guaranteed by \cref{def:aspread}. At the end, remove all columns from $A$ that violate (4).

    We start by analyzing how $X, Y, D$ change in one iteration of the algorithm, before the column removal phase. 
    For brevity, let $f$ be the indicator of $A \cap S(X, Y, D)$.
    If $A$ is not $(s+1, t, \eps)$-combinatorially spread, without loss of generality we can find subsets $X_1 \subseteq X$ and $D_1 \subseteq D$ with $|X_1| \geq 2^{-s-1}|X|$ and $|D_1| \geq 2^{-t}|D|$ where
    $$
        \E_{x \in X, z \in D}[\mbm{1}_{X_1}(x)\mbm{1}_{D_1}(z)f(x,x^{-1}z)] \geq (1+\eps)\E_{x \in X, z \in D}[f(x,x^{-1}z)] \cdot \E_{x \in X}[\mbm{1}_{X_1}] \cdot \E_{z \in D}[\mbm{1}_{D_1}],
    $$
    or equivalently after normalizing,
    $$
        \E_{x \in X_1, z \in D_1}[f(x,x^{-1}z)] \geq (1+\eps)\E_{x \in X, z \in D}[f(x,x^{-1}z)].
    $$
    In other words, 
    $$
        \frac{|A \cap S(X_1, Y, D_1)|}{|X_1||D_1|} \geq (1+\eps)\frac{|A \cap S(X, Y, D)|}{|X||D|}.
    $$
    We then recurse on $A \cap S(X_1, Y, D_1)$. The claim is that in at most $O(\eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha))$ iterations, we obtain sets $X^*, Y^*, D^*$ of size at least $|X^*|, |Y^*| \geq 2^{-O(s \eps^{-1} \log(1/\alpha)}$ and $|D^*| \geq 2^{-O(t\eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha))}|G|$ which satisfy (2) and (3). Otherwise, every iteration has failed to find a container where $A$ is spread, at which point we have the guarantee that
    $$
        \frac{|A \cap S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)|}{\delta_{X^*} \delta_{Y^*} \delta_{D^*}|G|^2} \geq (1+\eps)^{O(\eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha))} \cdot \alpha > 2.
    $$
    Our choice of $s,t$ implies that $|X^*|, |Y^*| \geq 2^{-O(s\eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha)})|G| \geq |G|/Q^{1/12}$, and similarly $|D^*| \geq |G|/Q^{1/12}$, so it follows from \Cref{eq:SXYD_deviation_qr} that $|S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)| \leq 2\delta_{X^*} \delta_{Y^*} \delta_{D^*}|G|^2$. Combining with the above inequality gives
    $$
        \frac{|A \cap S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)|}{|S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)|} > \frac{|A \cap S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)|}{2\delta_{X^*} \delta_{Y^*} \delta_{D^*}|G|^2} > 1,
    $$
    which is a contradiction. 

    To complete the proof of the lemma, we must guarantee that $A \cap S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)$ has lower--bounded columns. To achieve this, we will simply remove all of the columns which violate (4). More formally, now let $f$ be the indicator of $A \cap S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)$ and $\alpha^* = |A \cap S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)| /(\delta_{X^*} \delta_{Y^*} \delta_{D^*}|G|^2)$, and let $L = \{ y \in Y^* : \E_{x \in G}[f(x,y)] < (1-\eps^{1/2})\alpha^* \delta_{X^*} \delta_{D^*}\}$. We may assume $|L| \leq 4\eps^{1/2}|Y^*|$, as otherwise \Cref{lemma:llarge}\footnote{While \Cref{lemma:llarge} is stated for $X,Y,D$ contained in a subspace $W$, the statement and proof migrate identically to the case of containment in an arbitrary group $G$.} implies there exists a subset $Y^+ \subseteq Y^*$ with $\delta_{Y^+} \geq \delta_{Y^*}/2$ such that
    $$
        \frac{|A \cap S(X^*, Y^+, D^*)|}{\delta_{X^*} \delta_{Y^+} \delta_{D^*}|G|^2} > (1+\eps) \alpha^*,
    $$ 
    which contradicts the $(s,t,\eps)$-combinatorial spreadness of $A$ in $S(X^*, Y^*, D^*)$. Thus, we define $Y^+ \coloneqq Y \setminus L$ so that $|Y^+| \geq (1-4\eps^{1/2})|Y^*|$. Define
    $$
        \alpha^+ \coloneqq \frac{|A \cap S(X^*, Y^+, D^*)|}{\delta_{X^*} \delta_{Y^+} \delta_{D^*}|G|^2}.
    $$
    Note that $\alpha^+ \geq \alpha^*$ since we only deleted sparse columns. Additionally, we have $\alpha^+ \leq (1+\eps)\alpha^*$ by $(s+1,t,\eps)$-combinatorial spreadness. Thus, in terms of the new density $\alpha^+$, all of the columns with $y \in Y^+$ satisfy
    $$
        \E_{x \in G}[\mbm{1}_{S(X^*, Y^+, D^*)}(x,y)] \geq (1-\eps^{1/2})\alpha^* \delta_X \delta_D \geq (1-2\eps^{1/2})\alpha^+\delta_X \delta_D.
    $$

    We conclude by showing that $A$ is $(s, t, 5\eps^{1/2})$-combinatorially spread in the container $S(X^*, Y^+, D^*)$. 
    For clarity, define $F_1 : Y^* \times D^* \to \{0,1\}$ and $F_2 : X^* \times D^* \to \{0,1\}$ with $F_1(y,z) = f(zy^{-1}, y)$ and $F_2(x,z) = f(x,x^{-1}z)$. The $(s,t,5\eps^{1/2})$-combinatorial spreadness of $F_2$ follows easily, since for any $X' \subseteq X^*$ with size $|X'| \geq 2^{-(s+1)}|X^*|$ and $D' \subseteq D^*$ with size $|D'| \geq 2^{-t}|D^*|$, we have
    $$
        |A \cap S(X', Y^+, D')| \leq |A \cap S(X', Y^*, D')| \leq (1+\eps) \alpha^* \delta_{X'}\delta_{Y^*} \delta_{D'} |G|^2.
    $$
    This combined with the fact that $\delta_{Y^+} \geq (1-4\eps^{1/2})\delta_{Y^*}$ and $\alpha^+ \geq \alpha^*$ gives
    $$
        |A \cap S(X', Y^+, D')| \leq (1+5\eps^{1/2})\alpha^+ \delta_{X'}\delta_{Y^+} \delta_{D'} |G|^2,
    $$
    which is equivalent to $F_2$ being $(s+1, t, 5\eps^{1/2})$-combinatorially spread. Showing spreadness of $F_1$ is only slightly more subtle. For $Y' \subseteq Y^+$ with size $|Y'| \geq 2^{-(s+1)}|Y^*|$ and $D' \subseteq D^*$ of size at least $2^{-t}|D^*|$, we again have
    $$
        |A \cap S(X^*, Y', D')| \leq (1+\eps) \alpha^* \delta_{X^*}\delta_{Y'} \delta_{D'} |G|^2.
    $$
    Since $|Y^+| \geq (1-4\eps^{1/2})|Y^*| \geq |Y^*|/2$, this means that $Y'$ must have density at least $2 \cdot 2^{-(s+1)} = 2^{-s}$ in $Y^+$, which means $F_1$ is $(s,t,5\eps^{1/2})$-combinatorially spread.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Completing the proof}

In this short section, we combine the previous pieces we have developed to establish \Cref{thm:main_full_qr}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of \Cref{thm:main_full_qr}]
    We prove the contrapositive. Let $A \subseteq G \times G$ be a set of size $|A| = \alpha |G|^2$ for $\alpha \geq \exp(-c(\log Q)^{1/11})$ for some small enough $c > 0$.
    The proof proceeds by restricting $A$ to a large container $S(X, Y, D)$ where $A$ is combinatorially spread using \Cref{lem:obtaining_spreadness_qr}.
    Then, we will argue that combinatorial spreadness is enough to ensure bounded grid norms using \Cref{lemma:quasidensincr}. 
    At that point, we can apply \Cref{lem:sparsevonneumann} to show that $A$ contains many corners.

    Let $\eps$ be a sufficiently small constant, and let 
    $$
        s = O(\log(1/\alpha)^4)\quad\text{and}\quad t = O(\log(1/\alpha)^2)
    $$
    for large enough implicit constants. Our choice of $s,t$ guarantees that $s,t \leq O(\log Q / \log(1/\alpha))$.
    By applying \Cref{lem:obtaining_spreadness_qr} with $s,t,\eps_s = \Theta(\eps^4)$ small enough, there exist sets $X,Y,D \subseteq G$ satisfying the following properties: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The set $A$ is $(s,t,O(\eps^2))$-combinatorially spread in the container $S(X, Y, D)$.
    \item $|X|, |Y|, |D| \geq 2^{-O(\log(1/\alpha)^5)} |G|$. 
    \item The columns of $A \cap S(X, Y, D)$ are lower bounded; namely for all $y \in Y$, 
    $$
        \E_{x \in G}[\mbm{1}_{A \cap S(X, Y, D)}(x,y)] \geq (1-O(\eps^2))\alpha^* \delta_X \delta_D
    $$
    where $\alpha^* = \frac{|A \cap S(X, Y, D)|}{\delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D |G|^2} \geq \alpha$. 
\end{enumerate}

    Let $f$ denote the indicator of $A \cap S(X, Y, D)$, and define the functions $F_1 : Y \times D \to \{0,1\}$ with $F_1(y,z) = f(zy^{-1}, y)$ and $F_2 : X \times D$ with $F_2(x,z) = f(x,x^{-1}z)$. 
    We will argue that the combinatorial spreadness of $A$ in the container $S(X, Y, D)$ implies certain grid norms are bounded. In particular, we will show that
    \[ 
        \|F_1\|_{G(2,k)} \leq (1+\eps^2/36)\alpha^* \delta_X \quad\text{and}\quad \|F_2\|_{G(2,k)} \leq 2 \alpha^* \delta_Y
    \]
    for $k = O(\log(1/\alpha^*)/\eps^4) = O(\log(1/\alpha))$ a large enough integer. 
    Without loss of generality, assume the latter does not hold. 
    We want to apply \Cref{lemma:quasidensincr}, so it suffices to show that $X, Y, D$ are sufficiently dense, and that $Q^{-1}$ is sufficiently small. First, we have $|X|,|Y|,|D| \ge |G|/2^{O(\log(1/\alpha)^5)} \ge |G|/Q^{1/8}$ by the choice of $\alpha$. Secondly, we have
    $$
        Q^{-1} \leq 2^{-O(\log(1/\alpha)^{11})} \leq (\eps\alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D)^{O(\eps^{-2} \log(1/\alpha)^3 + \eps^{-1}\log(1/\alpha)\log(1/(\delta_X \delta_Y)))}.
    $$
    \Cref{lemma:quasidensincr} implies that there are $Y' \subseteq Y$ and $D' \subseteq D$ with $|Y'| \ge (\eps\alpha/2)^{O(k^2\log(1/\alpha))}|Y|$ and $|D'| \ge (\eps\alpha/2)^{O(\log(1/\alpha))}|D|$, and
    \[ 
        |A \cap S(X, Y', D')| \ge (1+\Omega(\eps^2))\alpha\delta_X|Y'||D'|. 
    \]
    This, however contradicts the $(O(\log(1/\alpha)^4, O(\log(1/\alpha)^2, O(\eps^2))$-combinatorial spreadness of $A$ in the container $S(X, Y, D)$. By our choice of $Q$, we have $Q^{-1} \leq 2^{-O(\log(1/\alpha)^6)} \leq (\eps \alpha \delta_X \delta_Y \delta_D)^{O(k)}$ so \Cref{lem:sparsevonneumann} implies that
    \[
        \Phi(f, f, f) \geq (1-O(\eps)) \alpha^3\delta_X^2\delta_Y^2\delta_D^2.
    \]
    In other words, $A$ contains at least 
    \[
        (1-O(\eps)) \alpha^3 \delta_X^2 \delta_Y^2 \delta_D^2 |G|^2 \geq 2^{-O(\log(1/\alpha)^{5})} |G|^3 > |G|^2
    \]
    many corners. Since there are at most $|G|^2$ trivial corners (i.e., triples $(x,y), (xg,y), (x,gy)$ with $g=\mbf{1}_G$), the result follows.
\end{proof}

\section{Almost Periodicity}\label{sec:almost-period}

We first state the key almost periodicity result which we require. This is proven as \cite[Theorem~5.4]{SS16}; we quote the statement from \cite[Theorem~8]{Mil24} (which while stated for $\mb{Z}/N\mb{Z}$ follows for general $G$ by changing each occurrence of $\mb{Z}/N\mb{Z}$ in the half page deduction from \cite[Theorem~5.4]{SS16} to $G$).

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:AP-input}
Let $\eps\in (0,1/2)$. Let $B_1, B_2, \dots$ be a $(d,\eta)$-small sequence of Bohr sets (see \cref{def:etasmall}).

Let $Y\subseteq B_1$ and $Z\subseteq B_2$ with $\beta = \E_{x\sim B_1}[\mbm{1}_{Y}(x)]$ and $\gamma = \E_{x\sim B_2}[\mbm{1}_{Z}(x)]$. Let $D\subseteq G$ be such that $|Y|\le |D|\le 2|B_1|$.

Then there exists a regular Bohr set $B'\subseteq B_2$ of dimension at most $d+d'$ where 
\[d' = O \left( \eps^{-4}\log(2\beta^{-1})^{3}\log(2\gamma^{-1}) \right)\]
and radius $r_2 \cdot (\eps \beta)/(24d^3d')$ and such that 
\[\Big|\E_{\substack{b\sim B'\\y\sim B_1\\z\sim B_2}}\mbm{1}_{D}(z-y + b) \mbm{1}_{Y}(y)\mbm{1}_{Z}(z) -\E_{\substack{y\sim B_1\\z\sim B_2}}\mbm{1}_{D}(z-y) \mbm{1}_{Y}(y)\mbm{1}_{Z}(z)\Big|\le \eps \beta \gamma.\]
\end{theorem}

We now give the proof of \cref{thm:ap+KM}.
\begin{proof}[{Proof of \cref{thm:ap+KM}}]
We first begin with essentially the dependent random choice argument of \cite{KM23}; we follow the proof as in \cite[Theorem~3.7]{FHHK24}. We let $B_3$ be a regular Bohr set with the same frequencies as $B_i$ and such that $r_3/r_2\in [\eta/2,\eta]$. Observe that 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\y\sim B_2\\z\sim B_3}}f_1(x-z)f_2(y + z)g(x+y)\ge (1+9\eps/10)\cdot \E[f_1] \cdot \E[f_2] \cdot \E[g].\]
Let $p = 1000\lceil k\log(1/\eps)/\eps\rceil$ and observe that 
\begin{align*}
\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\y\sim B_2\\z\sim B_3}}f_1(x-z)f_2(y + z)g(x+y)&\le (\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}f_1(x-z))^{p-1}\cdot \Big(\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1}}\Big(\E_{y\sim B_2}f_2(y+z)g(x+y)\Big)^{p}\Big).
\end{align*}
Via the definition of $p$, this implies that 
\[(1+7\eps/8)^p \cdot (\E[f_2]\E[g])^{p}\le \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\y_1,\ldots,y_p\sim B_2\\z\sim B_3}}\prod_{j=1}^pf_2(y_j+z)g(x+y_j).\]
Define 
\[\mc{E}_1(y_1,\ldots,y_p) = \mbm{1}\Big[\E_{x\sim B_1}\prod_{j=1}^pg(x+y_j)\le (\E[f_2]\cdot \E[g])^p\Big] \cdot \mbm{1}\Big[\E_{z\sim B_3}\prod_{j=1}^pf_2(y_j+z)\le (\E[f_2]\cdot \E[g])^p\Big]\]
and observe that 
\[(1+3\eps/4)^p \cdot (\E[f_2]\E[g])^{p}\le \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\y_1,\ldots,y_p\sim B_2\\z\sim B_3}}(1-\mc{E}_1(y_1,\ldots,y_p))\prod_{j=1}^pf_2(y_j+z)g(x+y_j).\]
We define 
\[\mc{E}_2(x,z) = \mbm{1}\Big[\E_{y\sim B_2}f_2(y+z)g(x+y)\le (1+5\eps/8) \cdot (\E[f_2]\cdot \E[g])\Big].\]
Observe that 
\begin{align*}
\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\y_1,\ldots,y_p\sim B_2\\z\sim B_3}}\mc{E}_2(x,z)&\prod_{j=1}^pf_2(y_j+z)g(x+y_j) \\ 
&\le 10^{-5}\cdot \eps \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\y_1,\ldots,y_p\sim B_2\\z\sim B_3}}(1-\mc{E}_1(y_1,\ldots,y_p))\prod_{j=1}^pf_2(y_j+z)g(x+y_j).\end{align*}
Then there exists $(y_1,\ldots,y_p)\in B_2^{\otimes p}$ such that $\mc{E}_1(y_1,\ldots,y_p) = 0$ and 
\begin{align*}
\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}\mc{E}_2(x,z)\prod_{j=1}^pf_2(y_j+z)g(x+y_j)\le 10^{-5}\cdot \eps \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}\prod_{j=1}^pf_2(y_j+z)g(x+y_j).\end{align*}
Let $H_1(x) =\prod_{j=1}^{p}g(x+y_j)$ and $H_2(z) =\prod_{j=1}^{p}f_2(y_j+z)$. By the definition of $\mc{E}_1$ we have that $\E[H_1]\ge (\E[f_1]\cdot \E[g])^p$ and $\E[H_2]\ge (\E[f_1]\cdot \E[g])^p$. The above conclusion is then equivalent to 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)\mc{E}_2(x,z)H_2(z) \le 10^{-5}\cdot \eps \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z).\]
If we now forget the precise details of the construction, we may assume that $H_1:B_1\to \{0,1\}$ and $H_2:B_3\to \{0,1\}$ with $\E[H_1]\ge (\E[f_1]\cdot \E[g])^p/2$ and 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)\mc{E}_2(x,z)H_2(z) \le 10^{-5}\cdot \eps \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z)\]
via \cref{lem:extract-cor}.

We now observe that 
\[\E_{y\sim B_2}f_2(y+z)g(x+y) = \E_{y\sim B_2}f_2(y)g(x+y-z)+O(\eta d).\]
Let $\mc{E}_3(t) = \mbm{1}[\E_{y\sim B_2}f_2(y)g(t + y)\le (1+9\eps/16) \cdot \E[f_2]\E[g]]$ and note that we have 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)\mc{E}_3(x-z)H_2(z) \le 10^{-5}\cdot \eps \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z).\]
This is equivalent to 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)(1-\mc{E}_3(x-z))H_2(z) \ge (1-10^{-5}\cdot \eps) \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z).\]
Observe that if $\E_{x\sim B_1}(1-\mc{E}_3(x))\le \E[H_1(x)]/2$ then 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)(1-\mc{E}_3(x-z))H_2(z)\le \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}(1-\mc{E}_3(x-z))H_2(z)\le 3/4 \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z)\]
which is a contradiction. 


We now apply \cref{thm:AP-input}. We first suppose that $\E_{x\sim B_1}(1-\mc{E}_3(x))\ge \E[H_1(x)]$. Then $(1-\mc{E}_3)$ will be $\mbm{1}_D$, $H_1$ will be $\mbm{1}_Y$, and $H_2$ will be $\mbm{1}_Z$. There exists a Bohr set $B'$ of dimension bounded by $d + d'$ with 
\[d'\ll \eps^{-4} p^{4} \cdot \log(1/(\E[f_2]\E[g]))^{4} \ll \eps^{-8}(\log(1/\eps))^4 \cdot k^{8}\]
and radius $r'\gg r_3 \cdot \eps \cdot (\E[f_2]\E[g])^{-O(k\log(1/\eps)/\eps)}/d^4$ such that 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3\\t\sim B'}}H_1(x)(1-\mc{E}_3(x-z+t)H_2(z) \ge (1-10^{-4}\cdot \eps) \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z).\]
Else we have that $\E_{x\sim B_1}(1-\mc{E}_3(x))\in [\E[H_1(x)]/2,\E[H_1(x)]]$ and observe that 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x+z)(1-\mc{E}_3(x))H_2(z) \ge (1-2\cdot 10^{-5}\cdot \eps) \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z).\]
Thus (taking $\mbm{1}_Z = H_2$, $\mbm{1}_D = H_1$ and $\mbm{1}_Y(y) = (1-\mc{E}_3)(-y)$) there exists $B'$ with the same properties as earlier such that 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3\\t\sim B'}}H_1(x+z+t)(1-\mc{E}_3(x))H_2(z) \ge (1-5\cdot 10^{-5}\cdot \eps) \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z).\]
Via a change of variable we get 
\begin{align*}
    \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3\\t\sim B'}}H_1(x)(1-\mc{E}_3(x-z-t))H_2(z) &= \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3\\t\sim B'}}H_1(x)(1-\mc{E}_3(x-z+t))H_2(z) \\
    &\ge (1-10^{-4}\cdot \eps) \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z);
\end{align*}
this is the same conclusion as earlier. 

This immediately gives the desired conclusion modulo unwinding definitions. Observe that we have that 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3\\t\sim B'}}H_1(x)(1-\mc{E}_3(x-z+t))H_2(z) \ge (1-10^{-4}\cdot \eps) \cdot \E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z).\]
Via the definition of $\mc{E}_3$, we obtain that 
\[\E_{\substack{x\sim B_1\\y\sim B_2\\z\sim B_3}}H_1(x)H_2(z)f_2(y)\Big(\E_{t\sim B'}g(x-z+y + t)\Big)\ge (1+17\eps/32) \cdot \E[H_1] \cdot \E[H_2]\cdot \E[f_2] \cdot \E[g].\]
Therefore there exists $x\in B_1, y\in B_2, z\in B_3$ such that 
\[\Big(\E_{t\sim B'}g(x-z+y + t)\Big)\ge (1+17\eps/32) \cdot \E[g]\]
as desired.
\end{proof}

