\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Sequential Recommendation (SR) has emerged as a prominent approach for modeling dynamic user preferences by analyzing historical interaction sequences~\cite{markovsr,transmarkov,markov,narm}. By focusing on predicting the next item of interest based on past behaviors, SR techniques capture the evolving nature of user interests within a single domain. However, SR faces several challenges, including data sparsity, where historical data for many items or users is insufficient, and domain bias, where models are overfitted to a specific domain or item set. These issues limit the ability of SR models to capture the full spectrum of user preferences, particularly when users interact with multiple domains. To overcome these challenges and improve recommendation quality, Cross-Domain Sequential Recommendation (CDSR)~\cite{pinet,Zhuangict,dagcn,kddsemi,cao2022contrastive,recguru} has been proposed. CDSR extends the SR framework by leveraging information across multiple domains, enabling a more holistic understanding of user preferences. By modeling interactions across diverse domains, CDSR systems can mitigate data sparsity and domain bias, leading to more accurate and comprehensive recommendations that reflect the user's diverse interests and behaviors across different contexts.

