Early efforts in CDSR, such as $\pi$-Net~\cite{pinet}, focused on modeling item interaction sequences within a single domain and proposed transferring learned representations to other domains using gated transfer modules. These early models aimed to enhance cross-domain learning by reusing knowledge acquired from one domain to improve recommendation performance in other domains. However, these methods were primarily concerned with capturing intra-sequence relationships within individual domains, failing to address the complexities of inter-sequence relationships that span across multiple domains. As a result, while these approaches helped to some extent in alleviating the data sparsity and domain bias issues, they still faced challenges in fully integrating information from multiple domains in a meaningful way. Subsequent work, such as MIFN~\cite{mifn}, made strides by incorporating external knowledge graphs to strengthen the connections between domains. By leveraging knowledge graphs, these models could better align domain-specific information and enrich the representations of items and users across domains. Despite these improvements, the focus remained on enhancing the relationships within each individual domain, often neglecting the rich interactions between sequences across domains. This limitation further perpetuates the problem of domain bias, which continues to affect the overall performance of CDSR models. Consequently, there remains a significant need for techniques that can more effectively capture both intra- and inter-sequence relationships across domains to achieve optimal performance in real-world recommendation systems.


Moreover, existing CDSR methods fail to fully exploit the rich visual information associated with items. Visual cues play a critical role in shaping user preferences, as users often form initial impressions of items based on their visual appearance, even when textual descriptions are similar. To address this limitation, we propose \textbf{LLM-Enhanced Multimodal Fusion for Cross-Domain Sequential Recommendation (LLM-EMF)}, a novel framework that leverages LLM knowledge to enhance textual information while integrating visual and textual embeddings to enrich item representations and model cross-domain user preferences. By leveraging the frozen CLIP model~\cite{radford2021learning}, we generate aligned image and text embeddings, capturing both intra- and inter-sequence relationships. Our hierarchical attention mechanism jointly learns single-domain and cross-domain preferences, enabling effective modeling of user behavior across diverse domains.


\textbf{Our key contributions are as follows:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We incorporate LLM knowledge to enrich item representations by generating additional item-related text information, thus enhancing item representation.
    \item We propose the CDSR framework, which seamlessly integrates visual, textual, and item embeddings, thereby enriching item representations and boosting recommendation performance through multimodal fusion.
    \item We conduct comprehensive experiments across four reorganized e-commerce datasets, showing that our approach, LLM-EMF, outperforms state-of-the-art baselines on all evaluation metrics.
\end{itemize}







\section{Related Work}




\subsection{Sequential Recommendation}
Sequential recommendation~\cite{SRs} serves as the fundamental framework for our approach, where user behavior is modeled as a sequence of time-sensitive items. The goal is to predict the likelihood of the next item in the sequence based on the user’s historical interactions. Initially, Markov chains were employed to capture the sequential dependencies within item sequences, as demonstrated in FPMC~\cite{rendle2010factorizing}, where the assumption of a first-order Markov process enabled the modeling of user preferences over time. As the complexity of user behavior grew, deep learning techniques were introduced to capture more intricate patterns of sequential interactions. Recurrent neural networks (RNNs)~\cite{GRU, LSTM}, convolutional neural networks (CNNs)~\cite{CNN}, and attention mechanisms~\cite{vaswani2017attention} have since been integrated into recommender systems~\cite{gru4rec, DIEN, Caser, kang2018self, DIN}, providing a more flexible and robust approach to modeling long-term dependencies and dynamic user preferences. These models can process sequential data more efficiently, making them highly effective in capturing the nuanced temporal evolution of user interests. In addition, methods like SURGE~\cite{SURGE} have leveraged metric learning techniques to reduce the complexity of item sequences, improving computational efficiency and enhancing the scalability of recommendation systems. This approach facilitates the efficient comparison of items in a lower-dimensional space, allowing for faster retrieval and better prediction accuracy in large-scale datasets.

More recently, works such as DFAR~\cite{lin2023dual} and DCN~\cite{lin2022dual} have sought to capture even deeper and more intricate relationships within sequential recommendation data. These models explore more sophisticated architectures, incorporating dual attention mechanisms and complex interaction learning to enhance the predictive power of the models, particularly in complex, noisy environments.

In this work, we extend the aforementioned techniques by applying cross-domain learning to sequential recommendation models. This novel approach facilitates the transfer of knowledge between multiple domains and LLM, allowing the model to leverage complementary information from different domains to improve recommendations. By doing so, we aim to enhance the generalization capabilities of sequential recommendation systems and make them more adaptable to a wide range of real-world applications.




\subsection{Cross-Domain Recommendation} Cross-domain recommender systems~\cite{CDR} have emerged as a promising solution to address the challenges of sparse data and cold-start issues commonly encountered in sequential recommendation tasks. These systems leverage data from multiple domains to enrich user modeling, providing a more comprehensive understanding of user preferences. Early models in this area were largely grounded in single-domain recommendation frameworks, under the assumption that auxiliary user behaviors from different domains could be used to improve the modeling process in the target domain~\cite{singh_relational_2008}. This assumption led to the development of various methods that utilized data from multiple sources to better predict user preferences within a specific domain. A key technique in cross-domain recommendation is transfer learning~\cite{Transfer}, which involves transferring learned representations (such as user or item embeddings) from the source domain to the target domain. This transfer of knowledge helps enhance the recommendation process in the target domain by incorporating relevant information from other domains. Notable examples of transfer learning-based approaches include MiNet~\cite{MiNet}, CoNet~\cite{CoNet}, and itemCST~\cite{itemCST}, among others. These methods have shown success in leveraging cross-domain knowledge to improve user-item interactions in the target domain. However, in practice, industrial platforms often aim to improve recommendation quality across multiple domains simultaneously, rather than focusing solely on enhancing the target domain while neglecting the source domain. This realization has led to the development of dual learning techniques~\cite{long_dual_2012, he_dual_2016,hou2024invdiff,li2025bridge,li2024towards2,li2024distinct,li2024towards}, which enable mutual improvement between both the source and target domains. These methods aim to simultaneously enhance the performance in both domains by modeling their interactions, and have been successfully applied to cross-domain recommender systems~\cite{DTCDR, DDTCDR}. By allowing both domains to benefit from each other's knowledge, dual learning has opened up new possibilities for more robust and scalable recommendation systems. Despite these advancements, many existing methods fail to account for the role of visual perceptual features, which can provide valuable context for user preferences. Furthermore, many approaches do not integrate multimodal fusion with attention mechanisms, which can significantly enhance the ability of models to focus on relevant information from different sources. The integration of multimodal signals, such as images, text, and other media, with attention mechanisms can improve the model’s understanding of complex user preferences across domains. These innovations hold great potential for advancing the effectiveness of cross-domain recommendation systems.

\subsection{LLM-based Recommendation System}
% Large Language Models~(LLMs) have been widely adopted as recommender systems to leverage item text features and enhance recommendation performance~\cite{geng2022recommendation, liu2023user,wu2023survey}. The majority of existing LLM-based recommenders operate in a tuning-free manner, utilizing pretrained knowledge to generate recommendations for the next item~\cite{sun2023chatgpt, wang2023recmind}. For instance, CHAT-REC~\cite{gao2023chat} employs ChatGPT to grasp user preferences and enhance interactive and explainable recommendations. GPT4Rec~\cite{li2023gpt4rec} utilizes GPT-2 to generate hypothetical "search queries" based on a user's historical data, which are then queried using the BM25 search engine to retrieve recommended items. Another research direction in LLM-based recommendation focuses on designing tuning strategies for specific subtasks. TALLRec~\cite{bao2023tallrec}, for example, employs instruction-tuning to determine whether an item should be recommended. BIGRec~\cite{bao2023bi} adopts a post-processing approach where recommendations are initially generated using LLM and then integrated with collaborative information through an ensemble method. RecInterpreter~\cite{yang2023large} and LLaRA~\cite{liao2023llara} proposes a novel perspective by considering the "sequential behaviors of users" as a new modality for LLMs in recommendation, aligning it with the language space. CoLLM~\cite{zhang2025collm} captures collaborative information using an external traditional model and maps it into the input token embedding space of LLM, creating collaborative embeddings for LLM utilization.

% Despite the significant progress achieved in the field of SR through traditional or LLM-based methods, these approaches tend to focus on a limited perspective of information formats. Consequently, there is an underutilization of information pertaining to the cold-start feature of CDSR, which is the primary focus of this paper. Moreover, although some previous works such as Tallrec~\cite{bao2023tallrec}, BIGRec~\cite{bao2023bi}, and LLM-Rec~\cite{tang2023one} recognize the cross-domain capabilities of LLMs, there is currently a lack of an LLM-based structure specifically optimized for CDSR.


Large Language Models (LLMs) have gained significant traction in recommender systems due to their ability to leverage textual features of items and improve recommendation performance~\cite{geng2022recommendation, liu2023user,wu2023survey,qiu2024tfb,qiu2025duet,qiu2025tab}. Most existing LLM-based recommenders operate without fine-tuning, relying on pretrained knowledge to predict the next item~\cite{sun2023chatgpt, wang2023recmind}. For example, CHAT-REC~\cite{gao2023chat} uses ChatGPT to understand user preferences and deliver interactive, explainable recommendations. Similarly, GPT4Rec~\cite{li2023gpt4rec} employs GPT-2 to generate hypothetical "search queries" from user history, which are then processed by the BM25 search engine to retrieve relevant items. Another line of research focuses on fine-tuning LLMs for specific recommendation tasks. TALLRec~\cite{bao2023tallrec}, for instance, applies instruction-tuning to decide whether an item should be recommended, while BIGRec~\cite{bao2023bi} combines LLM-generated recommendations with collaborative filtering through an ensemble approach. Additionally, RecInterpreter~\cite{yang2023large} and LLaRA~\cite{liao2023llara} introduce the idea of treating "sequential user behaviors" as a new modality for LLMs, aligning it with language representations. CoLLM~\cite{zhang2025collm} captures collaborative signals using an external model and integrates them into the LLM's token embedding space, enabling the use of collaborative embeddings.

Despite the advancements in Sequential Recommendation (SR) through traditional or LLM-based methods, these approaches often adopt a narrow view of information formats, leading to underutilization of critical features such as the cold-start problem in Cross-Domain Sequential Recommendation (CDSR). Furthermore, while some studies like TALLRec~\cite{bao2023tallrec}, BIGRec~\cite{bao2023bi}, and LLM-Rec~\cite{tang2023one} acknowledge the cross-domain potential of LLMs, there remains a gap in the development of LLM-based frameworks specifically tailored for CDSR. This paper aims to address these limitations by proposing a novel approach optimized for CDSR challenges.



