\subsection{{Motion Imitation Results}}
To benchmark ADD's motion tracking performance, we compare ADD against two established methods: DeepMimic and AMP \citep{deep_mimic, 2021-TOG-AMP}. DeepMimic, designed for precise motion tracking, relies on a manually tuned tracking reward function composed of multiple sub-terms,
\begin{equation}
    r_t = w^p r_t^p + w^{jv} r_t^{jv} + w^{rv} r_t^{rv} + w^e r_t^e + w^c r_t^c.
\end{equation}
{Each term is an exponentiated error (Eq. \ref{eq:exponential_reward_subterm}) with its own scale hyperparameter $\alpha^i$. Additionally, computing the joint velocity reward $r_t^{jv}$ requires joint-specific weighting, introducing further tuning complexity.} 
Similar to ADD, DeepMimic policies receive as input phase information in the form of target frames, which synchronizes the policy with a given reference motion. In contrast, AMP adopts an adversarial imitation learning framework designed to imitate the general style of a motion dataset, rather than exact motion tracking.
{While it is not designed for motion tracking, AMP is included in the comparisons, as ADD builds upon a similar adversarial learning framework. However, by adapting AMP's imitation objective, our approach can change the learning objective from general style imitation to accurate motion tracking. While many follow-up works extend DeepMimic and AMP with architectural innovations orthogonal to the imitation objective \citep{Luo2023PerpetualHC, tessler2024maskedmimic, 2022-TOG-ASE}, we focus on the core formulation of the imitation objective and therefore compare directly with DeepMimic and AMP. ADD can also be combined with these additional enhancements in the same way DeepMimic and AMP have been extended.}

To ensure a fair comparison, we disable the pose termination mechanism used in \citet{deep_mimic}, which terminates episodes if the character’s pose significantly deviates from the reference. Early termination is triggered only when the character makes improper contact with the ground. {Motion tracking performance is evaluated using DoF velocity tracking error, a good indicator of motion jitteriness, and position tracking error} $e^\mathrm{pos}_t$. $e^\mathrm{pos}_t$ measures the difference in the root position and relative joint positions between the simulated character and the reference motion:
\begin{align}
\label{Eq:pos_tracking_err}
e^\mathrm{pos}_t = \frac{1}{N^\mathrm{joint} + 1} \bigg( & \sum\limits_{j \in \mathrm{joints}} \left|\left|(\hat{\rvx}^j_t - \hat{\rvx}^\mathrm{root}_t) - (\rvx^j_t - \rvx^\mathrm{root}_t)\right|\right|_2 \nonumber \\
& + \left|\left|\hat{\rvx}^\mathrm{root}_t - \rvx^\mathrm{root}_t\right|\right|_2 \bigg).
\end{align}
Here, $\rvx^j_t$ and $\hat{\rvx}^j_t$ represent the 3D Cartesian position of joint $j$ from the simulated character and the reference motion,  respectively. $N^\mathrm{joint}$ denotes the number of joints in the character. Appendix \ref{supp:motion_tracking_additional_details} presents more details on the computational cost and hyperparameter settings.

Figures \ref{fig:motion_snapshots} and \ref{fig:EVAL_snapshots} showcase behaviors learned by the humanoid and EVAL robot trained via ADD, respectively. The behaviors can be best viewed in the supplementary video. ADD can closely imitate individual reference clips and larger motion datasets on different embodiments, successfully reproducing a diverse set of agile and acrobatic skills. This includes challenging parkour skills, such as Climb and Double Kong, which require particularly high motion tracking accuracy to replicate intricate contacts with the environment. A qualitative comparison of ADD against other methods is available in the supplementary video.

