Quantitative results of different methods, summarized in Table~\ref{tab:humanoid_motion_comp} and Table~\ref{tab:EVALMotionTracking}, show that AMP exhibits poor tracking performance, since the policies are trained using a general distribution-matching objective. Both ADD and DeepMimic demonstrate strong capabilities in tracking reference motions. However, there are important distinctions in their robustness and ease of deployment. The reliance on manually designed reward functions, to a degree, limits DeepMimic’s ability to effectively imitate a broad spectrum of motions, as it can be challenging to craft a general and effective reward function that can imitate a diverse variety of behaviors. For instance, DeepMimic-trained policies failed to reproduce the challenging parkour motions. For the Double Kong motion, DeepMimic-trained policies fail to jump over the boxes and learn to simply mimic running in place after tripping. In contrast, ADD successfully enables characters to clear the obstacles, replicating the jumps and intricate contacts. {Policies trained using DeepMimic also exhibit notable jitteriness when tracking the DanceDB dataset, whereas ADD consistently produces non-jittery motions across various reference motions, as reflected by its lower DoF velocity tracking errors. By automatically learning to balance different objectives, instead of relying on manually specified weights, ADD offers a more general and adaptive approach for imitating a diverse set of motions.}

{Furthermore, achieving high-quality motion tracking in DeepMimic often requires careful adjustment of a large number of reward parameters. This tuning process often entails significant domain knowledge and iterative tuning. Some sets of parameters might improve a subset of the objectives, but lead to performance degradation with respect to other objectives. Poorly chosen parameters can result in low motion quality and inferior sample efficiency, which presents a challenge for generalizing DeepMimic to new reference motions or embodiments. In comparison, ADD is able to automatically weigh the different objectives, reducing the burden of manual design. The sensitivity analysis of DeepMimic in Appendix~\ref{supp:dm_sensitivity_analysis} provides further evidence of these challenges, highlighting the limitations of DeepMimic’s reward design and the robustness of ADD.}

