However, ADD can struggle to reproduce certain highly dynamic motions (e.g., sideflip and roll), often converging to suboptimal behaviors. In the case of the sideflip, the policy learns to stand on the ground without executing the full flip. Prior methods address this by incorporating additional early termination heuristics \citep{deep_mimic, Luo2023PerpetualHC}, which we deliberately omit to get a clearer evaluation of the different imitation objectives. Additionally, ADD is less precise than DeepMimic on some forward locomotion skills, such as humanoid running and robot walking. The performance gap stems from ADD's difficulty in accurately tracking the root position, which is crucial for imitating forward locomotion behaviors. We hypothesize that this shortcoming arises due to the root position only accounting for three elements within the high-dimensional differential vector $\Delta$, and the gradient penalty restricts the discriminator from assigning significantly larger weights to the root position errors.

\section{{Non-Motion-Imitation Tasks}}
To demonstrate that ADD generalizes beyond motion imitation to standard RL benchmark tasks, we evaluate ADD on the multi-objective 2D Walker task \citep{2018DMControl}, and UniTree Go1 quadrupedal walking task \citep{legged-gym-pmlr-v164-rudin22a}.

\subsection{Training}
Policies are trained using PPO with either ADD or manually tuned reward functions from \citet{2018DMControl} and \citet{legged-gym-pmlr-v164-rudin22a}. The ADD training procedure largely follows Algorithm~\ref{alg:ADD}, with the key difference being the absence of a reference motion clip. Instead, the target state $\hat{\rvs}$ is composed of values such as the pre-defined target horizontal speed for the walker to reach or the real-time user-provided velocity commands for the quadruped. Additional details, such as the differential vector $\Delta$ constructed for each task, are available in Appendix \ref{supp:walker_additional_details} and \ref{supp:go1_additional_details}.

