	\paragraph*{Relevance of the \(\textbf{pp}\) post-processing step}
	To see the relevance of the \(\textbf{pp}\) post-processing step, consider the following example. Solving the \(\text{RP}^*(G)\) formulation for the input graph depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:graph_A} produces the feasible solution with the following decision variable values, which in turn lead to an infeasible/contradictory partition as depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:graph_B}:
	\begin{align*}
		x_{12} = 1, 
		x_{23} &= 1,\\
		x_{13} = 0, 
		x_{14} &= 0, 
		x_{24} = 0, 
		x_{34} = 0.
	\end{align*}

	\begin{figure}[!ht]
		\centering
		\subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.33\textwidth]{figures/MISC/graph_A.eps}\label{fig:graph_A}}
		\hfill
		\subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.33\textwidth]{figures/MISC/graph_B.eps}\label{fig:graph_B}}
		\hfill
		\subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.33\textwidth]{figures/MISC/graph_C.eps}\label{fig:graph_C}}
		\caption{(a) A weighted graph before partitioning. (b) The graph with a contradictory partition obtained by applying the \(\text{RP}^*(G)\) formulation without the post-processing step \(\textbf{pp}\). (c) The graph with a feasible and optimal partition obtained through the \(\text{RP}^*(G)\) formulation followed by the \(\textbf{pp}\) post-processing step to satisfy all transitivity constraints.}
		\label{fig:graphs_comparison}
	\end{figure}

	In the solution described above, the nodes 1, 3, and 4 are assigned to the same cluster because $x_{13}=x_{14}=x_{34}=0$. However, the solution, does not uniquely specify the cluster membership of node 2. From $x_{24} = 0$, one would consider node 2 is assigned to the same cluster as node 4 (and other nodes of the graphs). However, $x_{12} = 1$ and $x_{23} = 1$ imply that node 2 is not in the same cluster as nodes 1 and 3.

	The root cause of this contradiction is the absence of some constraints which were present in the classic formulation, but then were deemed redundant and removed from the formulation in $\text{RP}^*(G)$. The aforementioned solution violates two transitivity constraints in the classic formulation (corresponding to the triples 1-2-4 and 2-3-4):
	\begin{align*}
		x_{14} + x_{24} &\geq x_{12}, \\
		x_{24} + x_{34} &\geq x_{23}.
	\end{align*}

	The violation of these constraints is the reason that the solution does not uniquely specifies the cluster membership of node 2. The \(\textbf{pp}\) post-processing step amends the contradiction by adjusting the decision variables. The solution after the execution of the post-processing step becomes:
	\begin{align*}
		x_{12} &= 1, 
		x_{23} = 1,
		x_{14} = 1, 
		x_{24} = 1, 
		x_{34} = 1,\\
		x_{13} &= 0.
	\end{align*}

	This new solution corresponds to a feasible partition as depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:graph_C}. The solution has an objective value of 1.

