\section{Introduction}

Partial differential equations (PDEs) form the foundation of nearly all natural sciences and engineering, and solving them is a critical challenge. A major hurdle is approximating PDE solutions without succumbing to the curse of dimensionality \citep{E_2021, han2018solving}, which even plagues highly structured PDEs \citep{Beck_2021, berner2020numerically}. Recently, deep learning has emerged as a tool for solving PDEs, with roots in earlier work \citep{Lagaris_1998}. The rise in AI-driven approaches to the treatment of complex scientific problems has led to a new field known as ``AI for science''. Various deep learning methods have been developed to solve PDEs \citep{kaiser2021datadriven, erichson2019physicsinformed, Wandel_2021, li2022learning, salvi2022neural}.

Recent theoretical advances in operator learning provide further context for our work. %Notably, Boullé and collaborators have developed rigorous results on the sample complexity and generalization of neural PDE solvers. 
For example, \cite{nicolaselliptic} prove that certain elliptic PDEs can be learned with remarkably few data: they give an algorithm that achieves an exponential decay of error with respect to the number of input-output samples. A broader perspective is offered in the survey \citet{BOULLE202483}.

Among neural PDE solvers, Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) \citep{raissi2019physics} have emerged as a popular framework. PINNs can be effective for a wide range of PDEs, including the well-known Navier--Stokes equations \citep{ARTHURS2021110364, wang2020physicsinformed, Eivazi_2022}. They work by using neural networks (NNs) to approximate PDE solutions through a loss function that penalizes the network for not satisfying the initial and boundary conditions, as well as the differential operator. Since these terms are defined over different domains, PINNs minimize a multi-distribution loss, which sets them apart from typical machine learning models. Theoretical insights into the generalization properties of PINNs are still in their early stages \citep{kutyniok2020theoretical, guhring2019error, geist2020numerical}. Recent work \citep{Mishra_PINN_generalization_error} has provided initial error bounds applicable to all PDEs, but there are gaps between these bounds (which use quadrature-based terms) and the standard PINN framework. Despite extensive efforts \citep{DeRyck_Mishra_2024}, it remains unclear why minimizing the loss of standard PINN consistently leads to NNs that accurately approximate PDE solutions. 

The growing interest in PINNs has also exposed their struggles with even basic PDEs \citep{krishnapriyan2021characterizing, wang2022pinns}. To overcome these limitations, researchers have explored alternative gradient descent methods \citep{wang2020understanding} and hybrid approaches such as patching multiple solutions together \citep{hu2022XPINNs}. There also have been efforts to ameliorate the challenge of computing derivatives in high dimensional problems, resulting in variants that replace the derivative calculations needed for traditional PINNs by computation of certain stochastic estimators \citep{hu2023exactsolutions,he2023learning,hu2024hutchtrace}.

Another important challenge in understanding PINNs is the quantification of errors and uncertainties, especially when training on noisy observations \citep{karniadakisnoisyuq}. There are many varaints of PINNs, such as the Uncertain PINN \citep{ZHANGUPINN} and the Bayesian PINN \citep{YANGBPINN} that have been created for this purpose. This issue is particularly relevant in applications such as image super-resolution for medical diagnostics using PINNs \citep{frangi2023superresolution}. 

This paper presents a first-of-its-kind lower bound on the size of PINNs required to achieve small losses. Our theorems reveal a fundamental relationship between model complexity and the amount of data needed for high performance. Crucially, we show that if the loss is low, the model size must scale with the data to maintain accuracy. This finding challenges the assumption that noisy supervision is a ``free lunch''. Whether dealing with noisy initial conditions or solution samples, the model must surpass a critical size for these noisy inputs to enhance predictions. This result also has practical consequences for designing efficient and effective models in real-world scenarios, where noise is inevitable.




\subsection{The setup} Consider a PDE of the form
$$\setlength\abovedisplayskip{6pt}\setlength\belowdisplayskip{6pt}
\gL(u)=f, \quad\gB(u)= u_B,
$$
where $\gL$ is a differential operator that acts on functions on $\tilde{D} \times [0,T]$, for a domain $\tilde{D}\subset\mathbb{R}^d$, and $\gB$ is a boundary (or trace) operator that defines an evaluation of functions on the boundary of the domain. The goal of the PINN method is to find an approximate solution $u_\vw$ (paramtrized by weights $\vw$) such that $\gL(u_\vw)\approx f$ and $\gB(u_\vw)\approx u_B$. To do this, one typically uses three kinds of ``residual'' functions. The first is the PDE residual:
$$\setlength\abovedisplayskip{6pt}\setlength\belowdisplayskip{6pt}
R_{\mathrm{PDE}}[u_\vw](\vx,t) = \gL(u_\vw)(\vx,t)-f(\vx,t),\quad (\vx,t)\in \tilde{D}\times[0,T].
$$
For time-dependent PDEs with initial condition $u(\vx,0)=g(\vx)$, there is also a temporal initial value residual:
$$\setlength\abovedisplayskip{6pt}\setlength\belowdisplayskip{6pt}
R_{\mathrm T}[u_\vw](\vx) = u_\vw(\vx,0)-g(\vx),\quad \vx\in \tilde{D}.
$$
In the semi-supervised setting, there is also a loss term based on data sets, corresponding to an ``observable'' $\Psi$ in a subdomain $\tilde{D'}\times[0,T]$, on which (possibly noisy) observations $\tilde{g}$ are available of a functional $\Psi$ of the true solution. In real-world applications, it is rare to have access to noiseless data, so we include noisy in our supervised labels to capture the difficulty of observing data. This leads to the data or supervised residual:
$$\setlength\abovedisplayskip{6pt}\setlength\belowdisplayskip{6pt}
R_{\mathrm S}[u_\vw](\vx,t) = \Psi[u_\vw](\vx,t)-\tilde{g}(\vx,t),\quad (\vx,t)\in \tilde{D'} \times[0,T].
$$
In total, the PINN risk function is given as:
\begin{align}\label{eq:genpinn}\setlength\abovedisplayskip{6pt}\setlength\belowdisplayskip{6pt}
    R_{\mathrm{PINN}}(u_\vw) = \int_{\tilde{D}\times[0,T]}R_{\mathrm{PDE}}[u_\vw]^2\dd{\vx} \dd{t} + \lambda_0\int_{\tilde{D}}R_{\mathrm T}[u_\vw]^2\dd{\vx} + \lambda_s\int_{\tilde{D'}\times[0,T]}R_{\mathrm S}[u_\vw]^2\dd{\vx} \dd{t},
\end{align}
where $\lambda_0,\lambda_s>0$ are parameters used to weight the different residuals and losses. A natural choice is $\Psi=\gB,\tilde{g}=u_B$ and $\gD' = \partial \gD$ (the boundary of $\gD$), where the above penalizes $u_\vw$ not satisfying the boundary condition. Given a parameter space $\Theta$, the corresponding risk minimization/learning problem is to solve, $\min_{\vw\in\Theta} R_{\mathrm{PINN}}(u_\vw)$.

In this paper, we consider a fundamental open question:

\noindent\textbf{Question:}
{\em Given noisy observations with variance $\sigma^2$ at random locations within a PDE domain, under what conditions can a predictor achieve a supervised empirical PINN risk below $\sigma^2$?}

Answering this question is key to understanding whether PINNs can use noisy data to solve PDEs accurately. In this work, we focus on solving the Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman (HJB) PDE using supervised PINNs, and we derive the first necessary condition on model size and supervision for this desirable outcome. The HJB PDE is a nonlinear equation that governs the time evolution of a scalar function. It arises naturally in optimal stochastic control problems (see \cref{sec:control}) and is widely used in robotics research \citep{caoimpact,abu2005nearly,lewis2012optimal}. Its nonlinearity, high dimensionality, and importance make it an ideal test case for understanding the requirements PINNs must meet to successfully tackle complex PDEs under noisy conditions.

\subsection{Informal statement of the main result}


Our main result can be summarised as follows:

{\em 
Consider the PINN loss function for the HJB PDE. Let $\sigma^2$ be the average variance in the supervision data. If a predictor achieves an empirical risk $\mathcal{O}(\eta)$ below $\sigma^2$, then necessarily $d_N\log d_N\gtrsim N_s \eta^2$, where $N_s$ is the number of samples and $d_N$ is the number of trainable parameters of the PINN.}

This result shows that it is not necessarily useful to add more samples to the supervised loss term once a certain threshold is reached, but this threshold value may be increased by increasing the number of parameters in the model, such as the width of the net. In other words, to make use of a given number of noisy supervision samples, the model must have enough capacity—its size must exceed a certain threshold. We also show that a similar condition on model size applies when, instead of a data term, the PINN uses noisy samples of an initial condition $g$ at randomly chosen spatial points.

\cref{fig:intro-results} shows an illustration of the phenomenon, with full experimental details provided in \cref{sec:num_experiments}. Here, we have trained a number of PINNs at different sizes $d_N$ to solve an instance of the HJB PDE with $\sigma^2 = 0.5$ being the variance of the noise in the $N_s = 3276$ labels used in the supervision term. Beyond a critical value of the size of the nets, the empirical error achieved at the end of training is always below $\sigma^2$. Moreover, if $d_N$ is too small, the nets fail to attain training errors below $\sigma^2$, but there is consistent increase in the performance of the PINN as $d_N$ increases. We also observe an apparent scaling of $\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{d_N})$ consistent with our bounds.


\begin{figure}[th!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{PINN_plot.pdf}
\caption{The vertical axis is $1-{\rm (final ~training ~error)}$. The red dots represent the final training accuracy, while the blue dots show the training accuracy every 100 updates. The accuracy appears to grow like $\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{d_N})$ before plateauing at a level above one minus the variance.}\label{fig:intro-results}
